const lesson = (teach, operate, example, memory) => ({ teach, operate, example, memory });

export const lessons = {
  aws: [
    lesson('AWS IAM decides who may perform which action on which resource. Prefer short-lived role credentials and least-privilege policies; avoid long-lived access keys and broad wildcards.', 'Evaluate identity policies, resource policies, permission boundaries, SCPs, and explicit denies together. Verify with IAM Access Analyzer, policy simulation, and CloudTrail rather than guessing.', 'A marketplace gives its image processor permission to read one upload prefix and write one derivative prefix instead of granting the whole S3 account.', 'Identity, action, resource, conditions, then explicit deny.'),
    lesson('A VPC is a private routing boundary containing subnets, route tables, gateways, security groups, and network ACLs. Public IP addressing alone does not make a subnet public; a working route to an internet gateway does.', 'Trace packets hop by hop: DNS, source route, security group, NACL, destination route, and return path. Use VPC Flow Logs and Reachability Analyzer, and keep application tiers in private subnets.', 'An online marketplace exposes only its load balancer while application and database subnets have no direct inbound internet route.', 'A subnet is public because of its route, not its name.'),
    lesson('EC2 provides machine-level control; Lambda runs bounded event-driven functions without server management. Choose from workload duration, startup sensitivity, scaling shape, runtime control, and total cost—not fashion.', 'For EC2, test autoscaling, health replacement, patching, and capacity limits. For Lambda, bound concurrency, retries, timeouts, cold-start impact, and downstream connection pressure.', 'Campaign image resizing runs on Lambda with reserved concurrency, while a latency-sensitive long-running recommendation process stays on autoscaled EC2.', 'Lambda removes server ownership, not workload limits.'),
    lesson('S3 is a highly durable object store, not a POSIX filesystem. Design around object keys, whole-object writes, versioning, lifecycle rules, encryption, and explicit bucket access.', 'Block public access, prefer role-based access, enable versioning for recovery, and monitor 4xx/5xx errors. Diagnose wrong-region requests, policy denies, KMS permissions, and hot-key assumptions with request evidence.', 'Marketplace product images use immutable versioned keys, CloudFront delivery, lifecycle transition for originals, and a separate private upload prefix.', 'Objects have keys and versions; directories are only prefixes.'),
    lesson('RDS fits relational joins, constraints, and multi-row transactions; DynamoDB fits known key-based access patterns at predictable scale. Model the access pattern first because DynamoDB cannot rescue an undefined query model.', 'Protect either database with encryption, backups, restore tests, alarms, and bounded clients. For RDS watch locks, connections, replicas, and query plans; for DynamoDB watch partition distribution, throttling, and conditional writes.', 'Orders and payments remain in PostgreSQL on RDS, while idempotency records and campaign counters use DynamoDB conditional writes with TTL.', 'Choose by access and consistency needs, then operate the failure modes.'),
    lesson('CloudFront caches and terminates connections at edge locations close to users, reducing latency and origin load. Cache behavior depends on cache keys, headers, cookies, and TTLs, so a misconfigured key can either leak private data or destroy the hit rate.', 'Set cache policies from what actually varies the response, use origin access control instead of public buckets, add signed URLs or cookies for private content, and watch cache-hit ratio and origin request counts.', 'A marketplace serves product images and static assets from CloudFront with long TTLs and immutable filenames, while checkout responses stay uncacheable and personalized.', 'Cache what is shared; key on what actually varies.'),
    lesson('SQS provides durable point-to-point queues that decouple producers from consumers; SNS fans one message out to many subscribers. Both give at-least-once delivery, so consumers must be idempotent and handle retries and dead-letter routing.', 'Choose standard for throughput or FIFO for ordering and dedup, set visibility timeout above processing time, configure a dead-letter queue with a redrive policy, and alarm on queue age and DLQ depth.', 'Campaign order events publish to an SNS topic; an SQS queue per consumer (fulfillment, analytics, email) processes independently and dead-letters poison messages for inspection.', 'Queue decouples one consumer; topic fans out to many.'),
    lesson('CloudWatch collects metrics, logs, and alarms; the useful signals are the ones tied to user-visible impact, not every internal counter. Metric math, composite alarms, and structured logs turn raw data into an on-call signal.', 'Alarm on symptoms with runbooks, use embedded metric format or Logs Insights for high-cardinality debugging, set retention deliberately, and avoid alerting on noisy raw CPU when latency and error rate matter.', 'A marketplace alarms on p99 checkout latency and 5xx rate, then uses Logs Insights to find one failing dependency instead of paging on every CPU spike.', 'Alarm on user symptoms; log for the drill-down.'),
    lesson('Auto Scaling adjusts capacity to demand, while an Application or Network Load Balancer spreads traffic across healthy targets. Scaling that reacts too slowly or health checks that are too shallow both cause user-visible failures during campaigns.', 'Use target-tracking or predictive scaling from a leading metric, size cooldowns and warm-up correctly, make health checks test real dependencies, and drain connections on scale-in.', 'A marketplace scales its web tier on request-count-per-target ahead of a campaign, and the ALB stops routing to instances that fail a dependency-aware health check.', 'Scale on demand signal; route only to truly healthy targets.'),
    lesson('Cloud cost is an architecture property, not an afterthought. Most spend concentrates in a few resources, and the cheapest option depends on usage shape: on-demand, Savings Plans, Spot, and serverless each fit different predictability and interruption tolerance.', 'Attribute cost with tags and Cost Explorer, buy commitments only for steady baseline load, use Spot for interruptible work, right-size from utilization evidence, and set budgets and anomaly alerts.', 'A marketplace runs steady baseline capacity on a Savings Plan, bursts campaign image processing on Spot, and catches a runaway data-transfer bill through a cost anomaly alert.', 'Match pricing model to usage shape; measure before committing.'),
  ],
  azure: [
    lesson('Microsoft Entra ID authenticates identities; Azure RBAC authorizes actions on Azure resources. Use managed identities for workloads and scoped role assignments instead of storing client secrets.', 'Review inherited assignments from management group to resource, use Privileged Identity Management for elevated access, and inspect sign-in and activity logs. A token can be valid yet lack the required data-plane role.', 'A corporate application uses a managed identity to read one Key Vault and employees use Entra groups mapped to application roles.', 'Authenticate the identity; authorize the resource action.'),
    lesson('Azure Virtual Networks provide address and routing boundaries through subnets, route tables, NSGs, private endpoints, peering, and gateways. Peering connects networks but is not automatically transitive.', 'Trace DNS resolution, effective routes, NSG rules, private endpoint state, and return paths. Use Network Watcher connection troubleshoot and flow logs before changing rules.', 'An enterprise API reaches Azure SQL through a private endpoint while only Application Gateway has an internet-facing listener.', 'DNS chooses the destination; routes and NSGs decide whether packets arrive.'),
    lesson('App Service hosts web applications with managed platform operations; Functions runs event-driven units that scale by triggers. Choose by request lifecycle, scaling behavior, deployment needs, and dependency limits.', 'Use deployment slots, health checks, managed identity, application settings, and explicit scale limits. For Functions, control retries, poison messages, timeout, cold starts, and downstream connections.', 'The employee portal runs on App Service; document conversion runs from a queue-triggered Function with idempotency and a dead-letter path.', 'App Service hosts an app; Functions reacts to events.'),
    lesson('Azure Storage separates blob, queue, file, and table workloads. Use the service whose consistency and access model matches the data, and prefer private endpoints plus Entra authorization over account keys.', 'Enable versioning or soft delete where recovery matters, rotate any unavoidable keys, and inspect storage diagnostics for authentication, throttling, and latency. Design lifecycle and redundancy from recovery objectives.', 'HR uploads land in a private Blob container, trigger scanning through Queue Storage, and move to a cool tier after the retention window.', 'Pick the storage behavior first, then the redundancy tier.'),
    lesson('Azure SQL provides relational constraints and transactions; Cosmos DB provides globally distributed, partitioned document access with selectable consistency. Cosmos design begins with partition key and request-unit economics.', 'For Azure SQL monitor query plans, blocking, connections, geo-replication, and restore drills. For Cosmos monitor hot partitions, RU throttling, consistency choice, indexes, and multi-region failover.', 'Payroll remains in Azure SQL, while a globally read employee directory uses Cosmos DB partitioned by tenant with bounded-staleness reads.', 'Relational truth or partitioned global access: decide before choosing the product.'),
    lesson('Azure Monitor unifies metrics, logs, and traces; Log Analytics and KQL let you query them together. Application Insights adds request, dependency, and exception telemetry, but sampling and ingestion cost mean you should collect signal, not everything.', 'Alert on user-facing symptoms with action groups, correlate with distributed tracing, set data retention and daily caps deliberately, and use workbooks for recurring investigations.', 'An enterprise application traces a slow request in Application Insights to one downstream API dependency, confirmed by KQL over dependency telemetry before any code change.', 'Collect the signal that explains user impact, not every log line.'),
    lesson('Azure Service Bus provides enterprise messaging with queues, topics, sessions, and dead-lettering; Event Grid and Event Hubs suit reactive events and high-volume streams respectively. Delivery is at least once, so consumers must deduplicate.', 'Use sessions for ordered per-entity processing, set lock duration above handler time, configure dead-lettering and max delivery count, and monitor active and dead-letter message counts.', 'An enterprise order workflow uses a Service Bus topic with per-customer sessions so messages for one account process in order while other accounts proceed in parallel.', 'Pick the messaging service by ordering, throughput, and delivery guarantees.'),
    lesson('Azure Kubernetes Service manages the Kubernetes control plane while you own node pools, workloads, networking, and upgrades. Managed does not mean maintenance-free: version upgrades, node scaling, and pod security remain your responsibility.', 'Use managed identity and workload identity instead of secrets, separate system and user node pools, enable the cluster autoscaler, and keep the cluster version within support with tested upgrades.', 'An enterprise application runs stateless APIs on an AKS user node pool with workload identity to reach Key Vault, and drains nodes gracefully during rolling upgrades.', 'AKS runs the control plane; you still operate the workloads.'),
    lesson('Azure Key Vault stores secrets, keys, and certificates behind access policies or RBAC, with soft delete and purge protection. The goal is that no secret lives in code, config files, or pipeline variables in plaintext.', 'Grant workloads managed-identity access to only the secrets they need, enable rotation and expiry, use references instead of copies, and audit access through diagnostic logs.', 'An enterprise application reads its database connection string from Key Vault at startup via managed identity, and rotates it on a schedule without redeploying code.', 'Secrets live in the vault; workloads fetch them by identity.'),
    lesson('Azure cost depends on choosing the right service tier and pricing model for the workload. Reserved Instances and savings plans cut steady spend, spot VMs suit interruptible work, and serverless suits spiky load, but each trades flexibility for savings.', 'Tag resources for showback, use Cost Management budgets and anomaly alerts, right-size from Advisor recommendations, and reserve only capacity with proven steady baseline usage.', 'An enterprise workload reserves its steady App Service and SQL capacity, runs nightly batch on spot VMs, and catches an unexpected egress bill through a budget alert.', 'Match tier and commitment to the workload; measure before reserving.'),
  ],
  gcp: [
    lesson('Google Cloud IAM binds principals to roles on resources. Prefer predefined roles, workload identity, and service-account impersonation; avoid service-account keys and project-wide primitive roles.', 'Trace organization policies, inherited bindings, conditions, and deny policies. Use Policy Troubleshooter, audit logs, and IAM Recommender to prove access and safely reduce it.', 'A Cloud Run catalog service impersonates a narrowly scoped service account to read one bucket without shipping a JSON key.', 'Principal plus role plus resource, constrained by policy.'),
    lesson('A Google Cloud VPC is global, while subnets are regional. Routes, hierarchical firewall policies, Cloud NAT, load balancers, private service access, and DNS determine connectivity.', 'Check name resolution, effective routes, firewall rules, NAT ports, load-balancer health, and return traffic. Connectivity Tests and VPC Flow Logs provide evidence before rule changes.', 'Global users reach an external HTTPS load balancer; regional Cloud Run and database traffic stay on private paths.', 'Global network, regional subnets, explicit paths.'),
    lesson('Cloud Run is managed request- or event-driven container compute; GKE gives Kubernetes control and ecosystem flexibility. Cloud Run is simpler until scheduling, networking, sidecars, or platform policy truly require a cluster.', 'For Cloud Run bound instances, concurrency, timeout, cold starts, and database connections. For GKE own upgrades, requests, limits, autoscaling, disruption, security, and cluster observability.', 'A stateless global read API runs on Cloud Run; a GPU inference platform with custom scheduling and service mesh runs on GKE.', 'Start with managed containers; buy cluster control only when required.'),
    lesson('Cloud Storage holds immutable-style objects with classes, lifecycle, retention, encryption, and IAM. It is not a shared disk and object names do not create real directories.', 'Use uniform bucket-level access, versioning or retention where required, signed URLs for bounded sharing, and audit logs. Diagnose policy, KMS, location, and lifecycle behavior from logs.', 'A media service stores originals under immutable keys, delivers through Cloud CDN, and expires temporary renditions automatically.', 'Object key, access policy, lifecycle, recovery.'),
    lesson('Cloud SQL provides managed relational databases; Spanner provides horizontally scalable relational semantics and synchronous replication. Spanner is justified by scale and availability requirements, not by a desire to avoid schema design.', 'Cloud SQL needs connection pooling, query tuning, replicas, backups, and failover drills. Spanner needs good primary-key distribution, transaction awareness, instance sizing, and latency measurement across regions.', 'Account billing stays on regional Cloud SQL, while a truly global ledger with strict consistency and proven scale uses Spanner.', 'Pay for global consistency only when the requirement proves it.'),
    lesson('Pub/Sub is a global, at-least-once messaging service that decouples publishers from subscribers with push or pull delivery. Consumers must be idempotent, and ordering requires ordering keys within a region.', 'Set ack deadlines above processing time, use dead-letter topics with max delivery attempts, monitor subscription backlog and oldest unacked age, and use exactly-once delivery only where its cost is justified.', 'A data service publishes ingestion events to Pub/Sub; a subscriber processes them idempotently and dead-letters malformed messages after five failed attempts.', 'Decouple with at-least-once; make consumers idempotent.'),
    lesson('Cloud Monitoring and Cloud Logging collect metrics, logs, and traces; SLO-based alerting on error budgets beats alerting on raw resource use. Log-based metrics and Trace connect symptoms to causes.', 'Define SLOs and alert on burn rate, use structured logging with severity, sample traces, and set log retention and exclusion filters to control cost without losing signal.', 'A data service alerts when its 99.9% availability error budget burns too fast, then uses Trace to find the slow backend call responsible.', 'Alert on error-budget burn, not on raw CPU.'),
    lesson('BigQuery is a serverless columnar analytics warehouse that separates storage from compute and bills by bytes scanned or by slots. Query cost and speed depend on partitioning, clustering, and selecting only needed columns.', 'Partition by date and cluster by common filters, avoid SELECT *, use approximate functions and materialized views where acceptable, and set cost controls with per-query byte limits.', 'A data service partitions its events table by day and clusters by tenant, so a dashboard query scans one day for one tenant instead of the whole table.', 'You pay for bytes scanned; partition, cluster, and project narrowly.'),
    lesson('Secret Manager stores secrets with versioning and IAM-controlled access, so credentials never live in code or images. Access is granted per secret to workload identities, and old versions can be disabled or destroyed.', 'Grant least-privilege access per secret, enable rotation, reference secrets at runtime rather than baking them in, and audit access through Cloud Audit Logs.', 'A Cloud Run data service reads its API key from Secret Manager at startup using its service account, and rotates the key by adding a new version without a redeploy.', 'Store secrets versioned; grant access per workload identity.'),
    lesson('Google Cloud cost concentrates in a few services, and committed use discounts, sustained use discounts, preemptible/Spot VMs, and serverless autoscaling each fit different workload shapes. BigQuery and egress are common surprise line items.', 'Attribute spend with labels and billing export to BigQuery, commit only steady baseline capacity, use Spot for batch, and set budgets with alerts plus quota limits on runaway queries.', 'A data service commits its steady GKE baseline, runs analytics batch on Spot VMs, and catches an expensive unpartitioned BigQuery query through a budget alert.', 'Discount the steady baseline; alert on the spiky surprises.'),
  ],
  'javascript-typescript': [
    lesson('JavaScript runs one call stack per agent while the host schedules timers, I/O, and events. Promise callbacks enter the microtask queue, which drains before the next task and can starve rendering or I/O if abused.', 'Keep CPU-heavy work off the main or event-loop thread, measure event-loop delay, and split or move computation to workers. Never assume asynchronous means parallel.', 'A Node API streams network responses efficiently but moves image transformation to worker threads so one request cannot freeze every connection.', 'Stack now, microtasks next, tasks later.'),
    lesson('TypeScript checks static relationships before runtime; it does not validate JSON, database rows, or user input. Use narrow domain types internally and runtime validation at every untrusted boundary.', 'Avoid unchecked assertions and duplicated server/client types. Use discriminated unions for state, strict compiler settings, and type tests around reusable contracts.', 'A shared order type improves refactoring, while a runtime schema still rejects a malformed webhook before it reaches business logic.', 'Types protect code paths; validators protect boundaries.'),
    lesson('A Promise represents one eventual result. Use async/await for readable sequencing, Promise.all for independent work, allSettled for independent partial outcomes, and cancellation signals when abandoned work matters.', 'Handle rejection exactly once, bound concurrency, propagate AbortSignal, and avoid fire-and-forget work in request handlers. Measure queueing and dependency latency separately.', 'A product page fetches price and inventory concurrently but limits calls across a large catalog and cancels them when navigation changes.', 'Sequence dependencies; parallelize independence; cancel irrelevance.'),
    lesson('ES modules create explicit static imports and exports that tools can analyze. Keep module boundaries cohesive, avoid cycles and hidden side effects, and publish package exports rather than internal paths.', 'Check runtime format, resolution rules, tree shaking, and initialization order. A circular import may expose partially initialized bindings even when the type checker is happy.', 'A browser and Node application share pure domain modules while framework adapters remain separate entry points.', 'Dependencies point inward; side effects stay at the edge.'),
    lesson('A useful JavaScript test checks observable behavior at the cheapest reliable boundary. Unit-test pure rules, integrate real serializers and databases where contracts matter, and reserve browser tests for critical journeys.', 'Control time and randomness, avoid implementation-detail mocks, test rejected promises and cancellation, and make failures deterministic. Coverage is a map, not proof of correctness.', 'Checkout calculation has fast table-driven unit tests, a database integration test, and one browser purchase path instead of hundreds of brittle UI mocks.', 'Test behavior, boundary, failure, then journey.'),
    lesson('TypeScript generics express relationships between types so one function stays type-safe across many callers. Overusing conditional and mapped types makes signatures unreadable; the goal is inference callers never have to think about.', 'Constrain type parameters with extends, let inference flow from arguments, prefer a clear overload to an unreadable conditional type, and add type-level tests around reusable generic contracts.', 'A shared api<T>() helper infers the response type from a schema so every caller gets a typed result without casting, and a type test locks the inference in place.', 'Generics relate types; good ones disappear into inference.'),
    lesson('JavaScript errors propagate through throw and promise rejection; a thrown value can be anything, so callers should not assume it is an Error. Swallowed rejections and generic catch-all handlers hide the failures you most need to see.', 'Throw Error subclasses with context, handle rejection exactly once, never leave floating promises in handlers, and distinguish expected domain failures from unexpected bugs at the boundary.', 'A shared fetch wrapper throws a typed HttpError with status and URL, so a caller can retry a 503 but surface a 400 to the user instead of catching everything the same way.', 'Throw typed errors; handle rejection once; never swallow the unexpected.'),
    lesson('A closure captures variables from its defining scope, which keeps them alive as long as the closure is reachable. This powers callbacks and module state, but an accidental long-lived reference is a classic memory leak.', 'Be aware of what handlers, timers, and caches capture, remove listeners and clear intervals on teardown, avoid capturing large objects in long-lived closures, and confirm leaks with heap snapshots.', 'A dashboard subscription captures the component state in a closure; failing to unsubscribe keeps every old state object alive until a heap snapshot reveals the retained tree.', 'A closure keeps its captured scope alive until it is unreachable.'),
    lesson('Performance work in JavaScript starts with measurement, not intuition. The main-thread call stack, layout, and garbage collection each cause different symptoms, and micro-optimizing the wrong line wastes effort.', 'Profile with the browser Performance panel or Node profiler, look for long tasks and excessive allocation, batch DOM work, and confirm each change against a representative workload before keeping it.', 'A slow list is fixed by profiling to find a repeated JSON parse in a loop, hoisting it once, rather than prematurely memoizing render code that the profile showed was cheap.', 'Profile first; optimize the measured hot path only.'),
    lesson('Bundlers and toolchains transform, tree-shake, and split modules for the browser. Bundle size directly affects load performance, and a single large dependency or missing code-split can dominate the initial payload.', 'Analyze the bundle, prefer ES modules for tree-shaking, code-split by route, avoid importing whole libraries for one function, and set size budgets that fail the build when exceeded.', 'A shared app removes a 300 KB date library in favor of a small native Intl call after the bundle analyzer shows it dominates the main chunk.', 'Ship less: tree-shake, split by route, budget the bundle.'),
  ],
  python: [
    lesson('Python names reference objects; mutability, identity, equality, iteration protocols, and data-model methods determine behavior. Copying a container is shallow unless nested objects are explicitly copied.', 'Use immutable values for stable keys and boundaries, dataclasses for clear records, and profile allocations before optimizing. Watch mutable defaults and accidental aliasing.', 'A pricing service uses frozen value objects for money and avoids sharing a mutable default list across requests.', 'Names point to objects; assignment does not copy.'),
    lesson('Python type hints are optional static contracts consumed by tools; they do not enforce runtime input. Use precise public annotations, protocols for behavior, and validation where external data enters.', 'Run a strict checker in CI, narrow Optional values, avoid Any leakage, and keep runtime schemas separate from type-only models when trust differs.', 'A data API validates incoming JSON with a runtime model and exposes a typed protocol so storage adapters can be changed safely.', 'Hints guide developers; validators reject bad data.'),
    lesson('asyncio gives cooperative concurrency on one event loop. It helps many waiting I/O operations, not CPU-bound work, and every blocking call on the loop delays unrelated tasks.', 'Use structured task ownership, timeouts, cancellation, bounded semaphores, and async-compatible libraries. Move CPU work to processes or a dedicated worker system.', 'A service concurrently calls several APIs with per-call timeouts and a global concurrency cap, while video encoding runs in worker processes.', 'Async hides waiting, not computation.'),
    lesson('Python packaging defines build metadata, dependencies, importable modules, and reproducible installation. Use pyproject.toml, pinned application environments, isolated builds, and a deliberate public package surface.', 'Separate runtime and development dependencies, scan the resolved lock, test the built wheel, and avoid imports that depend on the working directory.', 'A data service builds one wheel in CI and promotes the same artifact through environments instead of rebuilding from an unpinned requirements file.', 'Build once; resolve explicitly; import predictably.'),
    lesson('Python tests should make state and dependencies visible. Use small unit tests for rules, fixtures for explicit setup, integration tests for adapters, and contract tests for external assumptions.', 'Freeze clocks only where needed, avoid giant shared fixtures, test exceptions and retries, and keep network calls out of unit suites. Use property tests when invariants matter more than examples.', 'A payment parser uses table tests for formats, property checks for round trips, and a sandbox integration test for provider compatibility.', 'Arrange visible state; assert behavior and failure.'),
    lesson('Generators produce values lazily with yield, so they process large or infinite sequences without holding everything in memory. The iterator protocol lets any object stream values, but a generator is single-pass and exhausts once consumed.', 'Use generators for pipelines over big files or streams, remember they cannot be reused after exhaustion, close them to release resources, and prefer itertools over hand-rolled loops.', 'A data-processing API streams a multi-gigabyte CSV through a generator pipeline that filters and transforms rows one at a time instead of loading the whole file into a list.', 'Generators stream lazily and run once; iterate, do not reload.'),
    lesson('Python exceptions signal errors up the call stack; catching too broadly hides bugs, and bare except even swallows KeyboardInterrupt. Prefer specific exceptions and let unexpected ones propagate to a boundary that logs them.', 'Raise precise exception types with context, use try/except/else/finally deliberately, chain with raise ... from, and never silence errors you cannot actually handle.', 'A data API catches a specific ValidationError to return a 400 but lets an unexpected database error propagate to the boundary handler that logs and returns 500.', 'Catch what you can handle; let the rest surface.'),
    lesson('The GIL means threads do not run Python bytecode in parallel, so threads help I/O-bound work while processes help CPU-bound work. Choosing the wrong model gives no speedup and adds complexity.', 'Use ThreadPoolExecutor for concurrent I/O, ProcessPoolExecutor or multiprocessing for CPU work, bound pool sizes, and protect shared state with locks or by not sharing it.', 'A data-processing API runs concurrent API calls on a thread pool but sends image resizing to a process pool so the GIL does not serialize the CPU-heavy work.', 'Threads for waiting, processes for computing.'),
    lesson('Python performance work begins with profiling, because the bottleneck is rarely where intuition says. Interpreter overhead, allocations, and algorithmic complexity cause different symptoms and different fixes.', 'Profile with cProfile or py-spy, fix the algorithm before micro-optimizing, use built-in C-backed operations and comprehensions, and consider NumPy or Cython only for proven hot numeric loops.', 'A slow report is fixed by profiling to find an O(n^2) membership check and switching a list to a set, rather than rewriting readable code that the profile showed was cheap.', 'Profile, fix the algorithm, then reach for C-speed tools.'),
    lesson('Pythons standard library covers most everyday needs, so adding a dependency for what the stdlib already does adds risk and maintenance. Modules like collections, itertools, functools, pathlib, and datetime prevent reinvented code.', 'Reach for the stdlib before pip, know the high-value modules, use dataclasses and enum for structure, and pull in a third-party package only when it clearly earns its weight.', 'A data service uses collections.Counter and itertools.groupby for aggregation instead of a heavyweight dependency, keeping the deployment small and the code obvious.', 'Check the stdlib before adding a dependency.'),
  ],
  java: [
    lesson('The JVM loads bytecode, manages heap objects, compiles hot paths, and collects unreachable memory. Latency problems can come from allocation rate, pauses, locks, native memory, or poor code—not simply “too little heap.”', 'Use GC logs, Java Flight Recorder, heap histograms, and thread dumps before tuning. Set container-aware limits and investigate retained objects rather than raising memory indefinitely.', 'A transaction service finds an unbounded cache retaining objects through a heap dump instead of masking it with a larger pod limit.', 'Measure allocation, retention, pause, and native memory separately.'),
    lesson('Java collections encode ordering, uniqueness, lookup, and concurrency guarantees. Choose from required operations and complexity; program to interfaces but understand the concrete implementation.', 'Define equality and hashCode consistently, avoid mutable map keys, size collections when evidence supports it, and use concurrent collections instead of unsynchronized shared HashMaps.', 'An idempotency service uses ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent for local coordination and a database constraint for cross-instance truth.', 'Choose the guarantee before the collection.'),
    lesson('Java concurrency coordinates threads through executors, futures, locks, atomics, and structured ownership. Shared mutable state creates visibility and race problems unless a happens-before relationship exists.', 'Bound executors and queues, propagate cancellation and deadlines, avoid blocking common pools, and inspect thread dumps plus saturation metrics. Prefer immutable messages and isolated ownership.', 'A transaction service uses a bounded executor for partner calls and rejects excess work rather than building an unlimited queue that exhausts memory.', 'Threads are finite; queues are promises about memory and latency.'),
    lesson('Streams express transformations over data; they are lazy until a terminal operation and are not automatically faster. Use them when the pipeline is clearer than a loop, not to hide stateful side effects.', 'Avoid parallel streams for blocking I/O or unknown common-pool workloads, preserve encounter-order only when needed, and profile allocation-heavy chains.', 'A report service uses a readable stream pipeline for filtering and grouping, but a simple loop for a hot allocation-sensitive parser.', 'Streams optimize clarity first; measure performance.'),
    lesson('Java testing should separate domain behavior from framework wiring. Use plain unit tests for rules, focused integration tests for persistence and serialization, and a few end-to-end paths.', 'Avoid mocking value objects and every collaborator, control clocks and executors, test transaction rollback and concurrency invariants, and use containers only where the real engine matters.', 'Ledger rules run in milliseconds without Spring; a PostgreSQL integration test proves locking behavior that an in-memory database cannot reproduce.', 'Test the rule plainly and the infrastructure honestly.'),
    lesson('Java generics give compile-time type safety over collections and APIs, but type erasure means the type argument is not available at runtime. Wildcards with extends and super control what a parameterized type can accept or produce.', 'Follow producer-extends, consumer-super for flexible APIs, avoid raw types, and pass a Class token when you genuinely need the runtime type erasure removed.', 'A transaction service exposes a Repository<T> with bounded wildcards so one query API works across entity types while the compiler still rejects a wrong-typed save.', 'Generics are compile-time; erasure removes them at runtime.'),
    lesson('Java distinguishes checked exceptions (recoverable, part of the signature) from unchecked runtime exceptions (usually bugs). Catching and ignoring, or wrapping everything in RuntimeException, both destroy the information a caller needs.', 'Throw specific exceptions, preserve the cause when wrapping, use try-with-resources for cleanup, and never catch Exception just to log and continue past a corrupt state.', 'A transaction service wraps a low-level SQLException in a domain PaymentStorageException with the cause attached, so the boundary can log the full chain and return a clear failure.', 'Checked for recoverable, unchecked for bugs; keep the cause.'),
    lesson('Records give concise immutable data carriers with generated equals, hashCode, and accessors; sealed types restrict which classes can implement an interface, enabling exhaustive pattern matching. Together they model closed sets of states cleanly.', 'Use records for value objects, sealed interfaces for known variants, and switch pattern matching so the compiler flags an unhandled case when a new variant is added.', 'A transaction service models each payment outcome as a record under a sealed PaymentResult interface, so a switch over results fails to compile if a new outcome is not handled.', 'Records carry values; sealed types close the set.'),
    lesson('Java I/O ranges from simple streams to non-blocking NIO channels and buffers. Blocking reads are simplest, while NIO and asynchronous channels scale to many connections without a thread per socket, at the cost of complexity.', 'Use try-with-resources for every stream, buffer appropriately, prefer java.nio.file for filesystem work, and choose NIO only when the connection count actually justifies it.', 'A transaction service streams a large export with buffered NIO file channels and try-with-resources, so memory stays bounded and file handles always close.', 'Match the I/O model to the connection count; always close resources.'),
    lesson('A Java build tool (Maven or Gradle) resolves dependencies, enforces versions, and produces reproducible artifacts. Transitive dependency conflicts and unpinned versions cause the classic works-on-my-machine failures.', 'Pin versions with a BOM or dependency management, inspect the dependency tree for conflicts, separate compile and runtime scopes, and scan dependencies for known vulnerabilities in CI.', 'A transaction service resolves a conflicting logging library by inspecting the Maven dependency tree and pinning one version through the dependency management section.', 'Pin versions, resolve conflicts, scan the tree.'),
  ],
  csharp: [
    lesson('C# distinguishes value and reference semantics, nullable state, generics, records, and pattern matching. Model invalid states out where practical, but still validate external input at runtime.', 'Avoid accidental boxing, mutable shared records, and null-forgiving operators that silence real uncertainty. Keep domain values explicit and benchmark hot generic paths.', 'A workflow service models each state as a discriminated-style record hierarchy so impossible transitions cannot be represented casually.', 'Represent state precisely; validate trust boundaries.'),
    lesson('LINQ composes queries, but execution may be deferred and the provider decides what can run remotely. An IEnumerable pipeline is in-process; IQueryable may become SQL and has different performance and translation limits.', 'Materialize deliberately, project only required fields, avoid N+1 navigation access, inspect generated SQL, and never enumerate an expensive query repeatedly.', 'An admin report projects directly to a DTO in SQL instead of loading entire entities and filtering them in memory.', 'Know who executes the query and when.'),
    lesson('async/await frees a thread while an operation waits; it does not make CPU work cheaper. Propagate Task and CancellationToken, avoid sync-over-async, and use bounded parallelism.', 'Observe all task failures, set deadlines, avoid Task.Run around naturally asynchronous I/O, and monitor thread-pool starvation plus dependency latency.', 'A workflow API awaits database and HTTP I/O, passes the request cancellation token, and limits concurrent partner calls.', 'Async releases waiting threads; cancellation releases abandoned work.'),
    lesson('.NET uses managed garbage collection, but unmanaged handles, large objects, pins, and retained references still require design. IDisposable expresses deterministic release of scarce resources.', 'Use using/await using, pool only measured hot resources, inspect allocation profiles and dumps, and avoid finalizers unless wrapping unmanaged ownership correctly.', 'A document service streams uploads and disposes each stream promptly instead of buffering every file on the large object heap.', 'GC handles memory reachability; IDisposable handles scarce ownership.'),
    lesson('C# tests should keep domain logic independent from ASP.NET and EF Core where possible. Unit-test rules, integrate the real database for translation and transactions, and exercise authorization at the HTTP boundary.', 'Avoid mocking IQueryable, test cancellation and concurrency, use deterministic clocks, and assert externally visible results rather than private calls.', 'A multi-step workflow has fast state-transition tests plus a PostgreSQL integration test proving its optimistic-concurrency behavior.', 'Plain domain tests, real boundary tests.'),
    lesson('C# generics are reified, so type arguments are available at runtime unlike Java erasure. Constraints (where clauses) let generic code call members safely, and the JIT can specialize value-type generics for performance.', 'Constrain type parameters to the smallest interface you need, prefer generic methods to object plus casting, and use in/out variance on interfaces where the direction of data flow allows it.', 'A workflow service uses a generic Result<T> with a where T : notnull constraint so success values stay strongly typed without boxing.', 'Constrain to what you call; C# generics keep their type at runtime.'),
    lesson('C# exceptions should represent exceptional conditions, not normal control flow, because throwing is expensive. Catching System.Exception broadly or swallowing errors hides real faults and corrupts state silently.', 'Throw specific exception types, preserve stack traces by rethrowing with throw not throw ex, use using for deterministic cleanup, and return result types for expected failures on hot paths.', 'A workflow service returns a Result for an expected validation failure but throws for an unexpected storage fault, rethrowing with throw to keep the original stack.', 'Exceptions for the exceptional; rethrow without losing the stack.'),
    lesson('C# collections encode different performance and semantics: List for indexed access, Dictionary for keyed lookup, HashSet for membership, and immutable and concurrent variants for shared state. The wrong choice hurts both clarity and speed.', 'Choose from required operations and complexity, pre-size when the count is known, use concurrent collections for shared mutation, and avoid LINQ in hot loops where a direct loop is clearer and faster.', 'A workflow service deduplicates event IDs with a HashSet for O(1) membership instead of repeatedly scanning a List.', 'Pick the collection by the operation you repeat most.'),
    lesson('Nullable reference types let the compiler track whether a reference can be null, turning many NullReferenceExceptions into compile-time warnings. The null-forgiving operator silences the checker and should be rare and justified.', 'Enable nullable context project-wide, annotate APIs honestly, handle null at boundaries where external data enters, and treat the ! operator as a claim you must be able to defend.', 'A workflow service annotates its repository to return Order? so callers are forced to handle the not-found case instead of hitting a null reference at runtime.', 'Let the compiler track null; do not silence it with !.'),
    lesson('.NET dependency injection resolves services by lifetime: transient, scoped, and singleton. Capturing a scoped service in a singleton, or storing state in a singleton, causes subtle cross-request bugs.', 'Register services against interfaces, choose lifetimes deliberately, keep singletons stateless and thread-safe, and create an explicit scope for background work that needs scoped services.', 'A workflow service injects its scoped DbContext into request handlers and creates a fresh scope per background job instead of capturing one in a singleton.', 'Match lifetime to state; never capture short-lived in long-lived.'),
  ],
  go: [
    lesson('A goroutine is a lightweight concurrently scheduled function managed by the Go runtime. It is useful for independent waiting work, but it does not remove synchronization, cancellation, backpressure, or ownership responsibilities.', 'Every goroutine needs a clear owner and exit path. Bound fan-out, pass context cancellation, inspect goroutine counts and profiles, and use the race detector; replace goroutines with a queue or worker service when work must survive process failure.', 'A network service runs one bounded worker per active task and cancels the group when the request deadline expires, preventing leaked background work.', 'Starting is cheap; lifetime and ownership are not.'),
    lesson('Channels transfer values and synchronization between goroutines. Use them to communicate ownership or coordinate pipelines; do not force every shared counter through a channel when a mutex is clearer.', 'Define who closes the channel, bound capacity from throughput and memory, select on cancellation, and avoid sends with no receiver. Test shutdown, slow consumers, and race conditions.', 'A log processor uses a bounded job channel, fixed workers, and context cancellation so overload becomes visible instead of consuming unbounded memory.', 'The sender sends; the owner closes; capacity is backpressure policy.'),
    lesson('Go interfaces describe behavior implicitly. Keep them small and usually define them where they are consumed; accept interfaces and return concrete types unless abstraction has a real caller.', 'Avoid speculative interfaces and typed-nil traps. Use compile-time conformance checks sparingly and test behavior with small fakes rather than mocking every method.', 'A service defines a two-method Clock interface only because deterministic time is needed in tests; its repository returns concrete domain values.', 'Interfaces belong to consumers, not implementers.'),
    lesson('context carries cancellation, deadlines, and request-scoped metadata across call boundaries. It is not a bag for optional parameters or long-lived application state.', 'Pass context as the first argument, never store it in a struct, call cancel functions, and ensure blocking work selects or checks cancellation. Trace deadline causes and goroutine growth.', 'When a client disconnects, the HTTP context cancels database and downstream calls so the service stops spending capacity on an abandoned response.', 'Context answers: is this work still wanted?'),
    lesson('Go tests favor observable behavior, table cases, small fakes, and the standard testing package. Add integration tests where real database or network semantics matter and fuzz parsers with strong invariants.', 'Run tests with -race, avoid time.Sleep synchronization, control time through dependencies, test cancellation and shutdown, and benchmark only representative hot paths.', 'A protocol parser uses table tests and fuzzing, while a service shutdown test waits on explicit signals rather than hoping a sleep is long enough.', 'Signal, do not sleep; test failure as a first-class path.'),
    lesson('Go treats errors as values returned explicitly, not exceptions. Wrapping with fmt.Errorf and %w preserves the chain so callers can inspect it with errors.Is and errors.As; ignoring a returned error is the classic silent bug.', 'Handle or return every error, wrap with context at each layer, define sentinel errors or types for cases callers must branch on, and reserve panic for truly unrecoverable programmer errors.', 'A network service wraps a failed downstream call with errors.Errorf so the handler can use errors.Is to distinguish a timeout worth retrying from a client error worth surfacing.', 'Errors are values: handle them, wrap them, never ignore them.'),
    lesson('Go generics (type parameters) let one function or type work over many types with compile-time safety, using constraints to define allowed operations. They are for genuine reuse, not a reason to abstract prematurely.', 'Use constraints from the constraints package or your own interfaces, prefer concrete code until duplication is real, and remember generics cannot replace interfaces for runtime polymorphism.', 'A network service uses a generic Map[T, U] helper for slice transforms instead of copying the same loop for every element type.', 'Type parameters for real reuse; interfaces for runtime dispatch.'),
    lesson('Go slices are views over a backing array with length and capacity; appending past capacity reallocates, and two slices can share and unexpectedly mutate the same array. Maps are unordered and unsafe for concurrent writes.', 'Copy when you need an independent slice, pre-allocate with make when the size is known, never rely on map iteration order, and guard maps with a mutex or use sync.Map under concurrency.', 'A network service copies a request slice before caching it so a later append by the caller cannot mutate the cached data through the shared backing array.', 'Slices share a backing array; copy to isolate, pre-size to avoid churn.'),
    lesson('Go modules pin dependencies through go.mod and go.sum, giving reproducible builds and cryptographic verification. Minimal version selection means the build uses the lowest version that satisfies all requirements, which keeps upgrades explicit.', 'Commit go.sum, use semantic import versioning for v2+, vendor or verify in CI, and run govulncheck to catch known vulnerabilities in the resolved graph.', 'A network service pins a security fix by bumping one dependency in go.mod, and CI verifies go.sum and runs govulncheck before merge.', 'go.mod pins, go.sum verifies, upgrades stay explicit.'),
    lesson('Go performance work relies on the built-in pprof profiler and benchmarks rather than guesswork. Allocation rate, garbage-collection pressure, and lock contention are common latency causes that intuition misses.', 'Write benchmarks with the testing package, profile CPU and heap with pprof, reduce allocations on hot paths, and confirm each change against a benchmark before keeping it.', 'A low-latency service cuts p99 latency by using pprof to find a per-request allocation in a hot loop and reusing a buffer with sync.Pool.', 'Benchmark and profile; chase allocations and contention, not hunches.'),
  ],
  'web-fundamentals': [
    lesson('Semantic HTML gives content native meaning, keyboard behavior, form semantics, and an accessibility tree before JavaScript runs. Choose elements for purpose, then style them.', 'Use headings in order, labels for controls, buttons for actions, links for navigation, and landmarks for page regions. Verify with keyboard-only use and the browser accessibility tree.', 'A commerce product selector uses fieldset, legend, labels, and buttons, so it works with screen readers and without custom keyboard code.', 'Use the element whose built-in contract matches the job.'),
    lesson('CSS layout uses normal flow, flexbox for one-dimensional alignment, grid for two-dimensional placement, and intrinsic sizing to negotiate available space. Responsive design should follow content breakpoints rather than device names.', 'Prevent overflow with min-width: 0 on flexible children, use logical properties, test zoom and long content, and avoid fixed heights for text regions.', 'A product grid uses auto-fit and minmax so cards reflow naturally from small phones to ultrawide screens without JavaScript width checks.', 'Let content size first; constrain only where necessary.'),
    lesson('Accessibility means people can perceive, operate, understand, and reliably use the interface with diverse input and output methods. WCAG is a testable baseline, not a substitute for user testing.', 'Preserve focus order, visible focus, contrast, names, error associations, reduced motion, and touch target size. Test keyboard, screen reader smoke flows, zoom, and automated rules.', 'Checkout announces validation errors, moves focus only when necessary, and remains usable at 200% zoom with a keyboard.', 'Name it, focus it, announce it, and keep it operable.'),
    lesson('A browser resolves DNS, establishes transport and TLS, sends HTTP, parses streamed bytes, builds DOM and CSSOM, lays out, paints, and composites. Caching and connection reuse can matter more than JavaScript micro-optimizations.', 'Use DevTools waterfalls and server timing to locate DNS, connection, TTFB, transfer, or main-thread delays. Validate cache headers, compression, priorities, and third-party cost.', 'A slow storefront is fixed by caching immutable assets and reducing origin TTFB after the network waterfall shows JavaScript execution was not the bottleneck.', 'Follow the request from name lookup to pixels.'),
    lesson('Core Web Vitals measure user experience: LCP for loading, INP for interaction responsiveness, and CLS for visual stability. Field data matters because lab hardware and networks are only controlled samples.', 'Attribute LCP to its resource and server path, INP to event and rendering work, and CLS to unsized or late content. Segment real-user data by page and device, then set regression budgets.', 'A commerce page reserves image and promotion space, preloads only its true hero, and breaks a long click handler into smaller work, improving CLS, LCP, and INP.', 'Load the main thing, respond quickly, move nothing unexpectedly.'),
    lesson('HTML forms submit data natively with built-in validation attributes, keyboard support, and accessibility. Client validation improves feedback but never replaces server validation, because anything from the browser is untrusted.', 'Use appropriate input types and required/pattern attributes, associate labels and error messages, keep the native submit working, and re-validate every field on the server.', 'A commerce checkout uses type=email and required for instant feedback but the server rejects a malformed address again before creating the order.', 'Validate near the user for feedback, at the server for trust.'),
    lesson('Browser JavaScript manipulates the DOM, handles events, and calls network APIs, all on the main thread. Heavy synchronous work blocks rendering and input, so long tasks show up as jank and poor responsiveness.', 'Delegate events, batch DOM reads and writes to avoid layout thrash, debounce expensive handlers, and move heavy computation to a Web Worker or break it into smaller tasks.', 'A commerce page keeps filtering responsive by debouncing the search input and updating the DOM in one batched write instead of on every keystroke.', 'The main thread renders and handles input; do not block it.'),
    lesson('Web security depends on not trusting the client and controlling what the browser is allowed to do. XSS, CSRF, and mixed content are the common failures; Content Security Policy, same-origin policy, and CORS are the browser controls.', 'Escape output by context to prevent XSS, use anti-CSRF tokens or SameSite cookies, set a strict CSP, serve everything over HTTPS, and configure CORS to allow only trusted origins.', 'A commerce site blocks a reflected XSS attempt because it escapes user content and its CSP disallows inline scripts.', 'Never trust the client; escape output and lock down the browser.'),
    lesson('Browser and CDN caching avoid re-fetching unchanged resources. Cache-Control, ETag, and immutable filenames control freshness; a wrong policy either serves stale data or defeats caching entirely.', 'Fingerprint static assets and cache them immutably for a year, keep HTML short-lived or revalidated, use a CDN close to users, and validate with ETags where content may change.', 'A commerce site serves hashed CSS and JS with a one-year immutable cache while HTML revalidates, so repeat visits load instantly but deploys still take effect.', 'Cache immutable assets hard; revalidate the documents that change.'),
    lesson('Progressive enhancement builds a working baseline with HTML and server responses, then layers CSS and JavaScript for richer experiences. The page still functions when a script fails, on slow networks, or for assistive technology.', 'Make core flows work with plain forms and links, enhance with JavaScript for those who have it, avoid making essential content depend on client rendering, and test with scripts disabled.', 'A commerce checkout works as a normal multi-page form submission, and client-side validation and single-page transitions enhance it only when JavaScript loads successfully.', 'Working baseline first; enhancement is a bonus, not a requirement.'),
  ],
  react: [
    lesson('React renders a description of UI from props and state, then commits changes. Rendering must stay pure because React may render, pause, discard, or repeat work before a commit.', 'Keep side effects out of render, use stable keys for identity, and measure commits with the profiler. Do not add memoization until a measured re-render is expensive.', 'An operations dashboard derives filtered rows during render and uses row IDs as keys, so sorting does not move local row state to another record.', 'Render describes; commit changes; effects synchronize.'),
    lesson('State should live at the lowest common owner that needs to change it. Store minimal source-of-truth data and derive the rest; use server-state tools for remote caches rather than duplicating them in component state.', 'Model transitions explicitly, avoid mirrored props and deeply global state, and separate URL, server, form, and ephemeral UI state by lifetime.', 'A dashboard keeps filters in the URL, fetched incidents in a query cache, and the open popover locally instead of putting all three in one global store.', 'Place state by owner and lifetime.'),
    lesson('An effect synchronizes React with an external system such as a subscription, timer, network API, or imperative widget. If a value can be calculated during render or handled in an event, no effect is needed.', 'Declare every reactive dependency, clean up subscriptions, abort stale work, and make setup safe to repeat. Avoid effect chains that encode business transitions indirectly.', 'A live incident view subscribes by incident ID and unsubscribes on change; sorting the incident list remains a pure calculation with no effect.', 'No external system, usually no effect.'),
    lesson('Accessible React begins with semantic HTML and predictable focus, then adds ARIA only when native behavior cannot express the widget. Component reuse must preserve labels and keyboard behavior.', 'Generate stable IDs, forward accessible names, restore focus after dialogs, respect reduced motion, and test composed components—not just primitives.', 'A shared dialog traps focus while open, labels itself from its title, closes with Escape, and returns focus to the button that opened it.', 'Composition must preserve the browser’s accessibility contract.'),
    lesson('React performance work starts by locating user-visible delay. Reduce unnecessary work, component breadth, bundle cost, and expensive rendering before reaching for blanket memoization.', 'Profile slow interactions, virtualize genuinely large lists, transition non-urgent updates, keep state local, and verify gains with production-like data and INP.', 'A dashboard improves filtering by moving state closer to the table and virtualizing 20,000 rows; memoizing every button had shown no measurable benefit.', 'Measure interaction, find work, remove or defer it.'),
    lesson('Hooks let function components use state and lifecycle features, but only when called unconditionally at the top level in the same order every render. A custom hook extracts reusable stateful logic without sharing state between callers.', 'Follow the rules of hooks, keep dependency arrays honest, extract repeated logic into a named custom hook, and remember each call to a custom hook has its own independent state.', 'A dashboard extracts polling logic into a useIncidents hook so several components reuse the fetch-and-refresh behavior while each keeps its own data.', 'Call hooks unconditionally; custom hooks share logic, not state.'),
    lesson('Context passes data through the tree without prop drilling, but every consumer re-renders when the context value changes. It suits low-frequency global data like theme or auth, not high-frequency state, and composition often avoids it entirely.', 'Split contexts by update frequency, memoize the provider value, prefer passing components as children to reduce drilling, and reach for a state library only when context re-renders become a measured problem.', 'A dashboard puts the rarely changing current user in context but passes the fast-changing filter state through props and composition to avoid re-rendering the whole tree.', 'Context for stable globals; composition beats drilling for the rest.'),
    lesson('Data fetching in React must handle loading, error, and stale states, and avoid race conditions when inputs change. Suspense and a data library coordinate this better than ad-hoc effects that set state after a fetch.', 'Cancel or ignore stale responses, key requests by their inputs, show error and loading boundaries, and prefer a server-state cache over duplicating fetched data in component state.', 'A dashboard fetches the selected incident with a query library that dedupes requests and discards a stale response when the user quickly switches incidents.', 'Handle loading, error, and staleness; cancel the race.'),
    lesson('A controlled input keeps its value in React state so the UI is the single source of truth; an uncontrolled input lets the DOM hold the value. Controlled forms enable validation and derived UI but re-render on every keystroke.', 'Use controlled inputs when you need live validation or dependent fields, uncontrolled with refs for simple or performance-sensitive forms, and a form library once cross-field rules grow complex.', 'A dashboard filter uses a controlled input to update results live, while a large settings form uses uncontrolled inputs and reads values on submit to avoid per-keystroke renders.', 'Controlled for live logic; uncontrolled for simple, fast forms.'),
    lesson('An error boundary is a component that catches render-time errors in its subtree and shows a fallback instead of unmounting the whole app. It does not catch errors in event handlers, async code, or the server, which need explicit try/catch.', 'Wrap independent regions in boundaries so one widget failing does not blank the page, log the caught error, provide a recovery action, and handle async and event errors separately.', 'A dashboard wraps each panel in an error boundary so a failing chart shows a retry message while the rest of the dashboard keeps working.', 'Boundaries catch render errors per region; handlers still need try/catch.'),
  ],
  nextjs: [
    lesson('Server Components render on the server and can access server-only data without shipping their component code to the browser. Client Components are required for state, effects, event handlers, and browser APIs.', 'Place the client boundary as low as practical, pass serializable props, and never import secrets into client code. Inspect bundle output rather than assuming a file is server-only.', 'A SaaS settings page fetches account data in a Server Component and isolates one interactive theme control as a small Client Component.', 'Server by default; client at the interaction boundary.'),
    lesson('The App Router maps folders to layouts, pages, loading states, errors, route groups, and dynamic segments. Layouts preserve shared UI while navigation streams route segments.', 'Keep route files thin, validate dynamic parameters, use notFound and error boundaries intentionally, and understand that search parameters and cookies make routes dynamic.', 'A SaaS separates public and protected route groups while sharing one root theme layout and colocated loading/error states.', 'Folders define URL structure; route groups define organization without URL cost.'),
    lesson('Next.js caching has separate layers for request memoization, data, full routes, and the client router. Correctness starts by stating how fresh data must be and which mutation invalidates it.', 'Use explicit cache modes, revalidation, tags, and dynamic APIs deliberately. Never cache personalized or authorization-dependent data publicly, and test stale and invalidation paths.', 'A public article list revalidates by tag after publishing, while account billing reads dynamically and sends private no-store responses.', 'Cache only data whose staleness and audience are explicit.'),
    lesson('Route Handlers expose HTTP endpoints inside the App Router. They should validate input, authenticate independently, call application services, and return stable status and cache semantics.', 'Keep credentials server-only, enforce authorization on every request, avoid trusting tenant IDs from clients, and make mutation origin/idempotency rules explicit.', 'An answer endpoint validates the track and question ID, resolves the session, calls a library service, and returns private no-store JSON.', 'HTTP boundary validates; service decides; provider fetches.'),
    lesson('Next.js performance combines server response time, cache correctness, streamed rendering, client bundle size, image/font delivery, and interaction cost. Faster static output is not useful if it leaks or serves stale private data.', 'Measure route timing, RSC payload, JavaScript bundles, Web Vitals, and cache hit behavior. Optimize the dominant delay and keep a rollback for caching changes.', 'A content SaaS streams the page shell, caches public catalog data, keeps user data dynamic, and removes a large client-only dependency from the initial route.', 'Send less, stream sooner, cache safely, measure users.'),
    lesson('Server Actions let client components call server functions directly for mutations without a hand-written API route. They run on the server, so they must authenticate, authorize, and validate input exactly like any endpoint, and revalidate affected caches.', 'Mark actions with use server, validate and authorize every call, never trust arguments from the client, and call revalidatePath or revalidateTag after a successful mutation.', 'A SaaS settings form calls a Server Action that checks the session, validates the input, updates the account, and revalidates the settings route so the UI reflects the change.', 'Server Actions are endpoints: authenticate, validate, revalidate.'),
    lesson('Next.js data fetching happens in Server Components with async/await and in Server Actions for mutations. Where and how you fetch determines caching, streaming, and whether secrets stay on the server.', 'Fetch on the server by default, colocate data with the component that needs it, choose cache and revalidate options explicitly, and stream slow data with Suspense so the shell renders first.', 'A SaaS dashboard fetches account data in a Server Component and streams a slow usage widget in a Suspense boundary so the page shell appears immediately.', 'Fetch on the server; stream the slow parts.'),
    lesson('Authentication in the App Router combines session verification in middleware or layouts with per-request authorization in Server Components and actions. Middleware runs on every matched request, so it must be fast and must not be the only check.', 'Verify sessions server-side, use middleware for coarse redirects only, enforce real authorization at the data layer, and keep tokens in httpOnly cookies rather than accessible client storage.', 'A SaaS uses middleware to redirect signed-out users from protected routes, while each Server Action re-checks that the user owns the resource before mutating it.', 'Middleware redirects coarsely; the data layer authorizes for real.'),
    lesson('Next.js can render statically at build (SSG), revalidate incrementally (ISR), or render dynamically per request (SSR). The right choice depends on how fresh the data must be and whether it is personalized.', 'Use static for stable public content, ISR with revalidation for content that changes occasionally, and dynamic rendering for personalized or always-fresh data; never statically cache authorization-dependent output.', 'A content SaaS renders marketing pages statically, revalidates the public article list with ISR after publishing, and renders the account dashboard dynamically per request.', 'Match rendering to freshness and personalization needs.'),
    lesson('The Next.js Image component optimizes format, size, and lazy loading, and fonts can be self-hosted to avoid layout shift and third-party requests. Unoptimized images and late-loading fonts are common Core Web Vitals regressions.', 'Set width, height, and priority on above-the-fold images, use next/font to self-host and preload fonts, serve modern formats, and size images to their display dimensions.', 'A SaaS marketing page marks its hero image as priority and self-hosts its font with next/font, removing layout shift and improving LCP.', 'Size and prioritize images; self-host fonts to stop shift.'),
  ],
  angular: [
    lesson('Angular components combine a template, state, dependency boundary, and lifecycle. Keep presentation focused, use inputs and outputs for clear contracts, and move domain operations to services.', 'Prefer predictable data flow, track repeated items by stable identity, clean up external resources, and test rendered behavior rather than private methods.', 'An admin table component receives rows and emits selection while a service owns loading and authorization-aware operations.', 'Components present; services coordinate.'),
    lesson('Angular dependency injection resolves providers from hierarchical injectors. Scope determines sharing and lifetime, so root, route, and component providers can produce very different state behavior.', 'Use injection tokens for configuration, avoid service-locator patterns, and choose scope deliberately. Diagnose duplicated state by inspecting where the provider is declared.', 'A wizard provides draft state at the route boundary so each wizard instance is isolated while the API client remains a root singleton.', 'Provider location defines instance lifetime.'),
    lesson('RxJS Observables represent values over time with cancellation and composition. Operator choice defines concurrency: switchMap replaces, concatMap queues, mergeMap overlaps, and exhaustMap ignores while busy.', 'Handle teardown, errors, retry limits, multicasting, and backpressure expectations. Avoid nested subscriptions; model the stream with operators and test with controlled time.', 'Type-ahead search uses debounce plus switchMap so a new query cancels the stale HTTP request and only the newest result updates the view.', 'Choose flattening by the business concurrency rule.'),
    lesson('Reactive forms model controls explicitly and suit complex validation and dynamic structure; template forms suit simpler screens. Client validation improves feedback but never replaces server validation.', 'Keep cross-field rules at the right group, show errors accessibly, prevent duplicate submission, and map server errors back to controls without losing user input.', 'An enterprise onboarding form validates date ranges and asynchronously checks an identifier, while the API repeats every security-sensitive rule.', 'Validate near the user and again at the trust boundary.'),
    lesson('Angular change detection updates bindings when scheduled work or reactive signals indicate change. OnPush and signals reduce unnecessary checking only when state updates remain explicit and immutable enough to observe.', 'Profile before tuning, avoid mutating inputs invisibly, use trackBy, and keep expensive calculations out of templates. Verify interaction latency with realistic lists.', 'An administration grid uses OnPush, immutable row updates, and stable tracking, reducing checks without manual detectChanges calls scattered across components.', 'Make change visible; let the framework schedule rendering.'),
    lesson('Angular signals are reactive values that track their own readers, so the framework updates only what actually depends on a changed value. Computed signals derive state, and effects run side effects when dependencies change.', 'Use signals for component state, computed for derived values, and effects sparingly for genuine side effects; avoid writing signals inside computed and keep effects free of unrelated logic.', 'An admin portal replaces a chain of observables and manual change detection with a signal for filters and a computed signal for the visible rows, updating only the affected view.', 'Signals track their readers; derive with computed, act with effects.'),
    lesson('The Angular router maps URLs to components with nested routes, lazy loading, and guards. Guards decide whether navigation may proceed and resolvers pre-fetch data, so authorization and loading state belong in the routing layer.', 'Lazy-load feature routes to shrink the initial bundle, use guards for auth and unsaved-changes checks, prefer functional guards, and re-check authorization server-side rather than trusting the guard alone.', 'An admin portal lazy-loads its billing feature and uses a guard to block access for users without the billing role, while the API still enforces the same rule.', 'Routes lazy-load features; guards gate navigation, servers enforce.'),
    lesson('The Angular HttpClient returns observables and integrates with interceptors that can attach auth headers, retry, log, or transform requests and responses centrally. Interceptors run in order, so placement matters.', 'Add auth tokens and correlation IDs in an interceptor, handle 401 refresh and retry centrally, unsubscribe or use the async pipe to avoid leaks, and keep error mapping consistent across services.', 'An admin portal uses an interceptor to attach the auth token to every request and to redirect to login once when any request returns 401.', 'Interceptors centralize cross-cutting HTTP concerns in order.'),
    lesson('Angular state can live in component fields, services with signals or observables, or a store library. The right scope depends on how widely the state is shared and how complex the transitions are; global stores add boilerplate that small apps do not need.', 'Keep ephemeral UI state in components, share cross-component state through a service, and adopt a store only when many features coordinate complex state with a need for traceable updates.', 'An admin portal keeps a dialogs open flag local, shares the current user through a service signal, and uses a store only for the complex multi-step wizard.', 'Scope state by sharing and complexity; stores are a last resort.'),
    lesson('Angular testing separates component behavior, service logic, and integration. TestBed configures a testing module, the HttpTestingController fakes HTTP, and testing rendered behavior beats asserting private methods.', 'Test what the user observes, mock HTTP with the testing controller, use fakeAsync for timers and async flows, and keep unit tests off the real network.', 'An admin portal tests that submitting the onboarding form calls the service and shows the success message, faking the HTTP call with HttpTestingController.', 'Test observable behavior; fake HTTP, control time.'),
  ],
  nodejs: [
    lesson('Node.js runs JavaScript on an event loop while delegating many I/O operations to the operating system or worker pool. It excels at concurrent I/O but one long synchronous task can delay every request.', 'Measure event-loop delay, CPU, worker-pool saturation, and memory. Stream or offload heavy work, bound concurrency, and never confuse many connections with unlimited capacity.', 'An API gateway handles thousands of sockets but sends PDF rendering to worker processes so request routing remains responsive.', 'One loop coordinates; blocking one task delays all.'),
    lesson('Node streams process data incrementally and propagate backpressure when producers outpace consumers. pipe or pipeline handles flow and error propagation more safely than manually wiring data events.', 'Respect write return values, bound transforms, handle abort and errors, and avoid buffering entire uploads. Test slow consumers and partial failures.', 'A gateway streams a multi-gigabyte export from object storage to the client with pipeline instead of holding it in heap memory.', 'Chunks plus backpressure keep memory bounded.'),
    lesson('Good APIs expose stable resources and operations, validate at the boundary, use meaningful status codes, and make pagination, idempotency, versioning, and errors explicit.', 'Authorize every resource independently, cap request sizes and rates, document retry safety, and add correlation IDs. Compatibility matters more than internal elegance.', 'A payment creation endpoint requires an idempotency key and returns the same result after a client timeout and retry.', 'Define contract, trust boundary, retry behavior, and evolution.'),
    lesson('Node security depends on treating all inputs and dependencies as untrusted. Validate payloads, parameterize queries, encode output by context, protect secrets, and keep authorization server-side.', 'Set secure headers and cookies, limit parsers and uploads, scan dependencies, prevent SSRF with destination policy, and log decisions without sensitive data.', 'A webhook endpoint verifies the raw-body signature before parsing, rejects replayed timestamps, and never logs the shared secret.', 'Validate shape, verify identity, authorize action, limit impact.'),
    lesson('Observability connects logs, metrics, and traces through request context. Record latency, traffic, errors, and saturation at service and dependency boundaries, not every internal line.', 'Use structured fields, redact secrets and personal data, sample traces deliberately, and alert on user-impacting symptoms with runbooks.', 'A gateway trace shows latency is in one partner API while event-loop delay remains healthy, preventing an unnecessary Node rewrite.', 'Observe the request path and the constrained resource.'),
    lesson('Node error handling must cover synchronous throws, rejected promises, and error events on streams and emitters. An unhandled rejection or an uncaught exception can crash the process, and swallowing errors hides real faults.', 'Use async/await with try/catch, attach error handlers to streams and emitters, centralize error handling in Express/Fastify, and let the process crash and restart on truly unexpected errors rather than limping on.', 'An API gateway wraps each handler so a rejected downstream promise becomes a clean 502 instead of an unhandled rejection that crashes the worker.', 'Handle sync, async, and event errors; crash on the truly unexpected.'),
    lesson('Node modules use CommonJS or ES modules, and package.json declares dependencies, entry points, and scripts. Mixing module systems, unpinned versions, and bloated dependency trees cause subtle runtime and supply-chain problems.', 'Commit the lockfile for reproducible installs, separate dependencies from devDependencies, audit for vulnerabilities, and prefer a few well-maintained packages over many tiny ones.', 'An API gateway pins its dependencies with a committed lockfile and removes an unused transitive package flagged by npm audit before it reaches production.', 'Pin with the lockfile; keep the dependency tree small and audited.'),
    lesson('Node runs on one thread, so CPU-bound work blocks the event loop; worker threads run CPU work in parallel and clustering runs multiple processes to use all cores. Neither helps if the real bottleneck is a slow dependency.', 'Offload CPU work to worker threads, scale across cores with the cluster module or a process manager, share nothing between workers except through message passing, and confirm the bottleneck is CPU before adding them.', 'An API gateway moves PDF generation to a worker thread pool so request routing stays responsive while reports render in parallel.', 'Worker threads for CPU, clustering for cores; measure the bottleneck first.'),
    lesson('Configuration should come from the environment, not hardcoded values, so the same artifact runs in every environment. Secrets belong in a secret store or injected env vars, never in code or the image, and config should be validated at startup.', 'Read config from environment variables, validate and type it at boot with a schema, fail fast on missing required values, and keep secrets out of logs and source control.', 'An API gateway validates its required environment variables at startup and refuses to boot with a clear error if the database URL is missing, rather than failing on the first request.', 'Config from the environment, validated at startup, secrets never in code.'),
    lesson('Node testing spans fast unit tests for logic, integration tests for real database and HTTP behavior, and a few end-to-end paths. The built-in test runner or a framework works; the key is testing observable behavior, not mocks of everything.', 'Unit-test pure logic, use a real test database for data-layer behavior, test error and timeout paths, and avoid mocking so much that tests pass while production breaks.', 'An API gateway has fast unit tests for its routing rules and an integration test that runs a real request through the router against a test database.', 'Unit for logic, integration for real boundaries, test the failures.'),
  ],
  'spring-boot': [
    lesson('Spring dependency injection builds an application graph from beans. Constructor injection makes required dependencies explicit, supports immutability, and exposes cycles early.', 'Keep bean scopes deliberate, avoid field injection and service-locator access, and separate configuration from domain rules. A cycle usually signals confused ownership.', 'An order service injects repository and payment ports through its constructor, so unit tests use small fakes without starting Spring.', 'Dependencies are required inputs, not hidden globals.'),
    lesson('A Spring REST API maps HTTP contracts to validated request models and application operations. Controllers should translate transport concerns, not own transactions or business policy.', 'Use consistent errors, validation, pagination, idempotency, content types, and authorization. Never expose persistence entities as an accidental public contract.', 'An order controller validates the command, calls one application service, and maps a duplicate idempotency key to the documented response.', 'Controller translates; application service decides.'),
    lesson('A transaction defines one atomic database boundary, not a distributed guarantee. Spring proxy-based @Transactional behavior depends on call path, propagation, isolation, and exception handling.', 'Keep transactions short, avoid remote calls inside them, understand rollback rules, test locking on the real database, and use an outbox for reliable post-commit events.', 'Order creation writes the order and outbox event together; payment happens asynchronously rather than holding a database transaction across HTTP.', 'One database transaction cannot atomically own another service.'),
    lesson('Spring Security separates authentication from authorization through filter chains and a security context. Method and resource checks still need explicit ownership and tenant rules.', 'Use modern password hashing, short-lived tokens or secure sessions, CSRF protection where cookies authenticate, least privilege, and deny-by-default route rules.', 'A user with a valid token can read only orders belonging to the active organization, enforced again in the service query.', 'Valid identity does not imply permission to this resource.'),
    lesson('Spring Boot observability uses structured logging, Micrometer metrics, traces, health indicators, and actuator endpoints. Health should describe whether traffic can be served, not expose internals publicly.', 'Measure request and dependency latency, pool saturation, JVM/GC, errors, and queue depth. Protect actuator data and preserve trace context across async boundaries.', 'An order latency alert is traced to database pool exhaustion, confirmed by pool wait metrics before any scaling change.', 'Observe user symptom, dependency time, and saturated resource.'),
    lesson('Spring Boot externalizes configuration through properties, YAML, environment variables, and profiles, with a defined precedence order. Profiles let one artifact behave differently per environment without code changes.', 'Bind config to typed @ConfigurationProperties, validate it at startup, keep secrets in a vault rather than committed files, and use profiles for environment differences, not for hiding required values.', 'An order service binds its payment-gateway settings to a typed properties class and switches the sandbox endpoint on with a staging profile, never editing code between environments.', 'One artifact, typed config, profiles for environments.'),
    lesson('Spring Data JPA maps entities to tables and generates queries, but the ORM does not remove the database. Lazy loading, N+1 queries, and the persistence-context lifecycle cause the most common production surprises.', 'Fetch associations deliberately with join fetch or entity graphs, keep the transaction and persistence context scoped correctly, paginate large results, and inspect the generated SQL rather than trusting the abstraction.', 'An order service eliminates an N+1 query loading order line items by using an entity graph, confirmed by logging the generated SQL.', 'JPA still runs SQL; control fetching and the persistence context.'),
    lesson('Spring Boot error handling centralizes exception-to-response mapping with @ControllerAdvice and @ExceptionHandler, producing consistent error bodies. Leaking stack traces or mapping every error to 500 both hurt clients.', 'Map domain exceptions to appropriate status codes, return a consistent error structure such as ProblemDetail, log with correlation IDs, and distinguish client errors from server faults.', 'An order service maps a duplicate-order exception to 409 with a clear ProblemDetail body through a global handler instead of returning a raw 500 stack trace.', 'Map exceptions centrally to consistent, correct responses.'),
    lesson('Spring caching abstracts a cache behind @Cacheable and @CacheEvict over a provider like Redis or Caffeine. The hard parts are invalidation on writes, key design, and not caching data whose staleness or audience is unsafe.', 'Choose keys that capture what varies, evict or update on writes, set TTLs, avoid caching authorization-sensitive results, and monitor hit rate against the cost of staleness.', 'An order service caches product catalog reads with @Cacheable and evicts the entry on a price update so stale prices never reach checkout.', 'Cache the shared and stable; evict precisely on writes.'),
    lesson('Spring Boot testing offers slice tests (@WebMvcTest, @DataJpaTest) for fast focused checks and @SpringBootTest for full-context integration. Loading the whole context for every test is slow and hides boundary problems.', 'Unit-test domain logic without Spring, use slice tests for the web and data layers, use Testcontainers for real database behavior, and reserve full-context tests for a few critical paths.', 'An order service tests its pricing rules as plain unit tests, its repository with @DataJpaTest against a Testcontainers PostgreSQL, and one full checkout flow end to end.', 'Slice tests for layers, real DB for data, few full-context runs.'),
  ],
  'aspnet-core': [
    lesson('ASP.NET Core middleware forms an ordered request pipeline. Each component may act before and after the next delegate or short-circuit entirely, so order changes security and behavior.', 'Place exception handling, forwarding, authentication, authorization, routing, and endpoints deliberately. Keep middleware stateless unless its lifetime is safe.', 'A tenant context middleware runs after authentication but before authorization so policies evaluate the verified tenant rather than an untrusted header.', 'Pipeline order is part of the security model.'),
    lesson('ASP.NET Core DI supports transient, scoped, and singleton lifetimes. Scoped commonly means one request; injecting a scoped service into a singleton creates invalid lifetime ownership.', 'Use constructor injection, keep singletons thread-safe, avoid service-location, and create explicit scopes only for owned background work.', 'A background queue creates a scope per job to obtain DbContext instead of capturing a request-scoped context forever.', 'The longer-lived object must not own a shorter-lived dependency.'),
    lesson('A Web API should bind and validate transport models, authenticate and authorize, invoke application logic, and produce a stable HTTP contract. Minimal APIs and controllers share these responsibilities.', 'Use problem details, idempotency, pagination, cancellation, request limits, and explicit versioning. Keep domain and EF entities out of public responses.', 'A tenant invoice endpoint resolves tenant from the session, not the route payload, and passes RequestAborted into the service.', 'Bind, validate, authorize, execute, translate.'),
    lesson('EF Core tracks entities and translates LINQ to provider queries. Correctness and performance depend on query shape, transaction boundaries, concurrency handling, and the actual database provider.', 'Project required columns, avoid N+1 access, inspect generated SQL, use no-tracking for reads, and handle optimistic concurrency explicitly. Do not share DbContext across threads.', 'A billing list projects directly to DTOs and uses a row-version token to reject conflicting edits instead of silently overwriting them.', 'Query translation and unit-of-work lifetime are the real abstraction.'),
    lesson('ASP.NET authentication establishes a principal; authorization policies decide whether that principal may perform the action. Resource ownership and tenant membership must be checked using trusted server data.', 'Validate issuer, audience, lifetime, cookie properties, CSRF behavior, and key rotation. Prefer policy handlers for reusable resource rules and log denials without tokens.', 'A valid business user receives 404 for another tenant’s document because the repository query is organization-scoped and the policy requires membership.', 'Identity first; policy and resource scope second.'),
    lesson('ASP.NET Core configuration layers providers (appsettings, environment variables, secrets) with a defined precedence, and the options pattern binds sections to typed classes. Secrets in appsettings committed to source control are a common leak.', 'Bind to typed options with validation, use user secrets in development and a vault in production, layer environment-specific files, and validate required options at startup with ValidateOnStart.', 'A multi-tenant API binds its rate-limit settings to a typed options class validated at startup and reads its connection string from a vault, not appsettings.json.', 'Layer config, bind to typed options, validate at startup.'),
    lesson('ASP.NET Core error handling uses exception-handling middleware to turn unhandled exceptions into consistent ProblemDetails responses. Returning raw exceptions or a generic 500 for everything gives clients no way to react.', 'Use the exception handler middleware, map domain exceptions to status codes, return ProblemDetails, hide stack traces in production, and log with a correlation ID for the drill-down.', 'A multi-tenant API returns a 409 ProblemDetails for a concurrency conflict through central exception handling instead of leaking an EF Core exception to the client.', 'Central middleware maps exceptions to consistent ProblemDetails.'),
    lesson('ASP.NET Core supports in-memory and distributed caching plus response and output caching. Distributed cache (like Redis) is needed across instances, and the difficulty is invalidation and not caching per-user or authorization-sensitive data publicly.', 'Use IDistributedCache for multi-instance state, set expirations, invalidate on writes, scope cache keys to the tenant, and never output-cache personalized responses.', 'A multi-tenant API caches reference data in Redis with tenant-scoped keys and evicts on update so one tenant never sees another tenant’s cached values.', 'Distributed cache across instances; scope keys and invalidate on write.'),
    lesson('Background work in ASP.NET Core runs via IHostedService and BackgroundService for long-running loops, plus queued hosted services for deferred work. Doing slow work inside a request ties up a thread and hurts latency.', 'Move slow or scheduled work to a hosted service, create a DI scope per unit of work, handle cancellation on shutdown, and use a durable queue when the work must survive a restart.', 'A multi-tenant API queues invoice PDF generation to a BackgroundService that creates a scope per job, so the request returns immediately.', 'Defer slow work to hosted services with per-job scopes.'),
    lesson('ASP.NET Core testing offers fast unit tests, integration tests with WebApplicationFactory that run the real pipeline in memory, and a real database via Testcontainers. Testing through the pipeline catches middleware and authorization bugs unit tests miss.', 'Unit-test domain logic, use WebApplicationFactory to test routing, model binding, and authorization end to end, run EF Core against a real provider, and assert observable responses.', 'A multi-tenant API uses WebApplicationFactory to prove a request for another tenant’s invoice returns 404 through the full authorization pipeline.', 'Unit for logic, in-memory pipeline for the boundary, real DB for data.'),
  ],
  'django-fastapi': [
    lesson('Django forms/serializers and FastAPI models turn untrusted request data into validated types. Validation should cover shape and local invariants; business and authorization rules still belong in services.', 'Reject unknown or oversized inputs where appropriate, normalize deliberately, return stable field errors, and never trust client-supplied ownership fields.', 'An inference API validates model name and input size, then the service resolves the allowed tenant model from the authenticated account.', 'Parsing makes data shaped; authorization makes the action allowed.'),
    lesson('Django ORM and SQLAlchemy-style data access translate object operations into SQL. Convenience does not remove joins, indexes, transactions, connection limits, or N+1 behavior.', 'Inspect queries, select related data deliberately, paginate, keep transactions short, and test against the production database engine.', 'A customer portal removes hundreds of repeated queries with select_related and an index confirmed by EXPLAIN.', 'Objects are convenient; the database still executes SQL.'),
    lesson('Authentication proves identity; application authorization decides permissions and object ownership. Framework defaults help, but every resource query must preserve tenant and ownership scope.', 'Use secure cookies or validated tokens, CSRF protection for cookie-authenticated mutations, modern password hashing, rate limits, and consistent denial behavior.', 'A customer can retrieve an invoice only through a query filtered by both invoice ID and authenticated organization ID.', 'Never fetch broadly and authorize afterward when a scoped query can do both.'),
    lesson('Async Python helps when the entire path uses nonblocking I/O. Calling synchronous ORM, CPU work, or blocking libraries inside an async endpoint stalls the event loop.', 'Choose a consistent boundary, use thread adapters only knowingly, bound concurrency, propagate cancellation, and move durable or CPU-heavy work to workers.', 'FastAPI handles concurrent model-service calls asynchronously, while Django background exports run in a job worker and persist status.', 'Async is only as nonblocking as the slowest call path.'),
    lesson('Framework tests should cover plain business rules, request validation and authorization, database behavior, and a small number of full journeys. Select the cheapest layer that proves the risk.', 'Use real database transactions for ORM behavior, override dependencies explicitly, test async cancellation and permission failures, and avoid snapshots that hide incorrect details.', 'A portal has unit tests for invoice rules, API tests for tenant isolation, and one browser payment flow.', 'Test rule, boundary, persistence, then journey.'),
    lesson('Schema migrations evolve the database in versioned, reviewable steps. Django migrations and Alembic for SQLAlchemy generate and apply changes, but a careless migration can lock a large table or break running old code during deploy.', 'Review generated migrations, make schema changes backward compatible with the currently running code (expand then contract), avoid long locks on big tables, and test the migration on production-like data.', 'A customer portal adds a nullable column and backfills it in a separate step so the old and new code both work during the rolling deploy, then makes it non-null later.', 'Migrate in reviewable steps; expand before contract.'),
    lesson('Caching in Django and FastAPI reduces load on the database and slow calls, using the framework cache or Redis. The hard parts are invalidation on writes, choosing keys, and never caching per-user data in a shared public cache.', 'Cache expensive read-heavy queries, key by what varies, invalidate on write, set TTLs, and keep authorization-sensitive results out of shared caches.', 'A customer portal caches the public plan list in Redis with a short TTL and invalidates it when an admin changes pricing, while per-user invoices are never shared-cached.', 'Cache stable shared reads; invalidate on write, never share private data.'),
    lesson('Background tasks move slow or scheduled work off the request path using Celery, RQ, or FastAPI background tasks. Lightweight in-process background tasks do not survive a restart, so durable work needs a real queue and worker.', 'Use a durable task queue for work that must not be lost, make tasks idempotent, set retries and timeouts, and keep task status queryable so the user can see progress.', 'A high-throughput inference API queues long model jobs to Celery workers with idempotent retries and exposes job status, instead of blocking the request until the job finishes.', 'Defer slow work to durable, idempotent, retryable tasks.'),
    lesson('API design in Django REST Framework and FastAPI covers versioning, pagination, consistent errors, and backward compatibility. Breaking an existing contract without a version breaks every client that depends on it.', 'Version the API, paginate large collections, return consistent error shapes, deprecate fields gradually, and document the contract so clients can rely on it.', 'A customer portal introduces a v2 endpoint with a new response shape while keeping v1 working, so existing integrations keep functioning during the transition.', 'Version, paginate, keep contracts stable and errors consistent.'),
    lesson('Configuration and settings should come from the environment so the same code runs everywhere, with secrets kept out of the repository. Django settings and FastAPI settings classes both benefit from validated, environment-driven config.', 'Read settings from environment variables, validate them at startup (e.g. with Pydantic settings), keep DEBUG off and secrets in a vault in production, and separate per-environment values from code.', 'A customer portal loads its database URL and secret key from environment variables validated at startup, and never commits production secrets to settings.py.', 'Config from the environment, validated, secrets out of the repo.'),
  ],
  'sql-postgresql': [
    lesson('Relational modeling stores facts once where practical and uses keys, constraints, and relationships to preserve meaning. Normalize to protect correctness, then denormalize only for a measured read pattern with an update strategy.', 'Use primary, foreign, unique, check, and not-null constraints as the final integrity boundary. Review cardinality, lifecycle, tenancy, and audit requirements before choosing tables.', 'A subscription platform stores invoices and line items relationally, with immutable price snapshots and constraints preventing duplicate provider references.', 'Model facts, identity, relationships, and invariants.'),
    lesson('A PostgreSQL index is an ordered access structure that can avoid scanning or sorting, but it costs storage and write work. Column order, predicates, selectivity, and query shape decide whether it helps.', 'Use EXPLAIN ANALYZE with real distributions, match composite indexes to equality/range/order needs, remove redundant indexes, and monitor bloat plus write overhead.', 'An unpaid-invoice query uses a partial composite index on organization and due date, shrinking both query time and index size.', 'Index the query pattern, not every column.'),
    lesson('A transaction groups changes atomically and isolation defines which concurrent effects may be observed. PostgreSQL MVCC lets readers and writers coexist but does not eliminate lost updates or business races.', 'Keep transactions short, lock only required rows, order locks consistently, retry serialization failures, and enforce invariants with constraints where possible.', 'Two payment callbacks race, but a unique provider-event constraint and transactional state change make processing idempotent.', 'Atomicity groups changes; isolation controls interference.'),
    lesson('A query plan is PostgreSQL’s chosen execution strategy based on statistics and estimated costs. A slow query may result from bad cardinality estimates, missing access paths, spills, lock waits, or simply returning too much data.', 'Compare estimated and actual rows, inspect buffers, sorts, loops, and timing, refresh statistics, and verify parameter behavior. Do not force an index before understanding the plan.', 'An invoice report is slow because a nested loop repeats an underestimated lookup; extended statistics and a query rewrite fix the estimate and plan.', 'Read plan nodes as data flow: rows, loops, buffers, time.'),
    lesson('PostgreSQL concurrency combines MVCC snapshots, row and table locks, deadlock detection, and isolation levels. Blocking is often correct; unbounded blocking without visibility is not.', 'Set sensible timeouts, inspect pg_stat_activity and lock graphs, keep transaction scope small, use consistent lock order, and test contention with production-like concurrency.', 'Billing workers claim small batches with FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED, process independently, and expose queue age so throughput and starvation are visible.', 'Know who waits for which lock and for how long.'),
    lesson('Joins combine rows across tables and subqueries compute intermediate results; the join type (inner, left, etc.) and order affect both correctness and performance. A wrong join can silently drop or duplicate rows.', 'Choose the join type from the required rows, ensure join columns are indexed, prefer a join or EXISTS over a correlated subquery when the planner handles it better, and check the plan for accidental row multiplication.', 'A subscription platform lists customers with their latest invoice using a lateral join instead of a correlated subquery that ran once per row.', 'Pick the join for the rows you need; index the join keys.'),
    lesson('Window functions compute values across a set of rows related to the current row without collapsing them, unlike GROUP BY. They express running totals, rankings, and per-group comparisons that would otherwise need self-joins.', 'Use OVER with PARTITION BY and ORDER BY for per-group calculations, choose the right frame, and remember window functions run after WHERE but can be filtered with a wrapping query.', 'A subscription platform computes each customer’s month-over-month revenue change with LAG over a partition instead of joining the table to itself.', 'Windows compute across rows without collapsing them.'),
    lesson('PostgreSQL stores semi-structured data in JSONB and arrays with operators and indexes (GIN). They add flexibility, but overusing them recreates a schemaless store inside a relational database and loses constraints.', 'Use JSONB for genuinely variable attributes, index with GIN for containment queries, keep queryable stable fields as real columns, and validate structure with check constraints where it matters.', 'A subscription platform stores fixed billing fields as columns but keeps variable plan metadata in a JSONB column with a GIN index for attribute lookups.', 'Columns for stable facts; JSONB for genuine variability.'),
    lesson('Partitioning splits one large table into physical partitions by range, list, or hash, so queries and maintenance touch only relevant partitions. It helps very large tables but adds complexity and requires the partition key in queries to benefit.', 'Partition by a key present in most queries (often time), automate partition creation, keep partitions pruned, and confirm the planner prunes partitions with EXPLAIN.', 'A subscription platform partitions its audit-log table by month so retention drops old partitions instantly and recent-date queries scan one partition.', 'Partition by the key you filter on; prune old partitions cheaply.'),
    lesson('Backups protect against data loss, but an untested backup is only a hope. PostgreSQL supports logical dumps and physical base backups with WAL for point-in-time recovery; the recovery objective (RPO/RTO) drives the strategy.', 'Automate backups, ship WAL for point-in-time recovery, encrypt and store copies off-site, and regularly restore to a scratch instance to prove the backup and measure recovery time.', 'A subscription platform runs continuous WAL archiving and rehearses a point-in-time restore quarterly, confirming it can recover billing data to any minute within the retention window.', 'A backup you have not restored is not a backup.'),
  ],
  'mongodb-nosql': [
    lesson('MongoDB document modeling groups data that is read and changed together. Embed bounded owned data; reference independently growing or shared data, because documents have a 16 MB limit and unbounded arrays become operational debt.', 'Design from access patterns, atomicity needs, cardinality, growth, and lifecycle. Add schema validation even when fields evolve.', 'A catalog embeds a bounded set of product attributes but stores high-volume reviews separately with product references and pagination.', 'Embed togetherness; reference independent growth.'),
    lesson('MongoDB indexes support query predicates and sort order but increase write cost and memory pressure. Compound order should reflect equality, sort, then range patterns rather than field popularity.', 'Use explain executionStats, inspect keys versus documents examined, create unique or partial indexes for invariants, and remove redundant indexes after workload evidence.', 'A catalog query uses a compound category, availability, and price index that both filters and sorts without an in-memory sort.', 'Index the full access pattern and verify scan efficiency.'),
    lesson('An aggregation pipeline transforms documents stage by stage. Put selective matches early, project only needed fields, and understand when sorting, grouping, lookup, or unwind expands memory and cardinality.', 'Inspect the explain plan, indexes, stage output sizes, spill behavior, and limits. Precompute stable expensive summaries when freshness permits.', 'A sales dashboard filters tenant and date using an index before grouping, instead of unwinding the entire global order collection.', 'Reduce rows early; expand and group only what remains.'),
    lesson('MongoDB consistency depends on replica-set behavior, read concern, write concern, sessions, and whether an operation spans documents. Single-document writes are atomic; multi-document transactions add coordination and cost.', 'Choose concerns from acceptable data loss and staleness, use retryable idempotent operations, test primary failover, and avoid reading from lagging replicas for read-after-write requirements.', 'A catalog tolerates slightly stale search reads, while inventory reservation uses majority writes and a guarded atomic update on one stock document.', 'State the stale-read and acknowledged-write contract.'),
    lesson('MongoDB scales through replicas for availability and reads, and sharding for data distribution. A shard key must distribute writes while supporting routing; an ever-increasing hot key can bottleneck one shard.', 'Measure working set, connections, replication lag, chunk balance, document growth, and scatter-gather queries. Sharding does not repair a poor schema or missing indexes.', 'A global catalog shards by a hashed tenant-aware key while keeping tenant-scoped queries targeted and avoiding one hot ingestion partition.', 'Distribute load without destroying query locality.'),
    lesson('MongoDB schema validation enforces structure with JSON Schema rules even though documents are flexible. Without validation, a flexible schema drifts into inconsistent shapes that break queries and application assumptions.', 'Define required fields and types with a validator, choose moderate or strict validation level, evolve rules alongside migrations, and reject or warn on documents that violate the contract.', 'A product catalog adds a validator requiring a name and price on every product so a buggy import cannot insert malformed documents that break the storefront.', 'Flexible does not mean unstructured; validate the contract.'),
    lesson('MongoDB multi-document transactions give ACID guarantees across documents, but they add coordination cost and are unnecessary when good document modeling keeps related data in one atomic document.', 'Model so common updates hit one document, use transactions only for genuine cross-document invariants, keep them short, and make retryable operations idempotent for transient errors.', 'A catalog reserves stock with a single atomic document update, and uses a transaction only when it must debit inventory and write an order record together.', 'Model for single-document atomicity; use transactions sparingly.'),
    lesson('Change streams let applications subscribe to inserts, updates, and deletes in real time using the replica-set oplog. They enable reactive pipelines and cache invalidation without polling, but consumers must handle resume tokens and duplicates.', 'Persist the resume token to continue after a restart, make handlers idempotent, filter the stream server-side, and account for the stream reflecting only committed majority writes.', 'A product catalog invalidates its search cache by subscribing to a change stream on the products collection instead of polling for updates.', 'React to committed changes; persist resume tokens, dedupe handlers.'),
    lesson('Data migrations in MongoDB reshape documents as the schema evolves. Because there is no enforced schema, migrations must handle old and new shapes coexisting, often with lazy migration on read plus a background backfill.', 'Support both shapes in code during transition, backfill in batches to avoid load spikes, make the migration idempotent and resumable, and verify counts before removing old-shape handling.', 'A catalog adds a new price structure by reading both old and new shapes, backfilling documents in batches, then dropping the old-shape code once all documents are migrated.', 'Handle both shapes, backfill in batches, then clean up.'),
    lesson('Operational monitoring of MongoDB watches replication lag, connections, cache hit ratio, slow queries, and oplog window. A shrinking oplog window or growing replication lag are early warnings of trouble under load.', 'Monitor the slow-query log and profiler, track working-set-to-RAM fit, alert on replication lag and connection saturation, and watch the oplog window so a lagging secondary can still catch up.', 'A product catalog catches a missing index early because the profiler flags a collection scan before it saturates the primary under peak read traffic.', 'Watch lag, connections, cache fit, and slow queries.'),
  ],
  'redis-caching': [
    lesson('Cache-aside loads missing data from the source and writes it to cache; write-through updates cache with the write; write-behind delays persistence and risks loss. Select a pattern from consistency and failure requirements.', 'Define key ownership, TTL, invalidation, stampede protection, and fallback. Treat the database as truth unless the design explicitly makes Redis authoritative.', 'A read-heavy product API uses cache-aside with jittered TTL and request coalescing so one expiry does not create thousands of database reads.', 'Every cache needs truth, expiry, invalidation, and failure behavior.'),
    lesson('Expiration limits staleness and memory, but TTL is not synchronization. Fixed simultaneous expiry can create a stampede; no expiry can preserve incorrect data indefinitely.', 'Add jitter, proactive refresh for hot keys, explicit invalidation for critical writes, and metrics for hit rate, age, eviction, and fallback latency.', 'Product summaries expire around five minutes with random jitter, while price changes publish immediate invalidations.', 'TTL bounds age; invalidation follows important change.'),
    lesson('Redis strings, hashes, sets, sorted sets, streams, and other structures provide different atomic operations. Choose the smallest structure matching access and concurrency needs, not the most feature-rich one.', 'Bound cardinality, estimate memory overhead, use atomic server operations or scripts for invariants, and avoid dangerous full-keyspace commands.', 'A leaderboard uses a sorted set for rank updates and range reads instead of reading every score into application memory.', 'Choose by operation: lookup, membership, rank, queue, or stream.'),
    lesson('A cache creates a second copy of data, so perfect consistency is rarely free. Define whether stale reads, missed invalidations, duplicate work, or temporary source reads are acceptable.', 'Use versioned keys or events where needed, make writes idempotent, prevent stale repopulation races, and test reorder, delay, and cache loss.', 'A price update increments a version used in the cache key, preventing an older in-flight request from overwriting the newly cached value.', 'Name the tolerated stale window and the repair path.'),
    lesson('Redis is an optional accelerator unless explicitly designed as a durable system. Applications must handle timeout, failover, eviction, restart, and overload without turning a cache incident into a total outage.', 'Set short timeouts, cap retries, use circuit breaking, protect the primary database from fallback storms, and monitor memory, evictions, latency, replication, and connection saturation.', 'When Redis fails, an API serves a bounded subset from PostgreSQL under tighter rate limits instead of retrying every cache request indefinitely.', 'Fail open or closed deliberately, and protect the system of record.'),
    lesson('A distributed lock in Redis coordinates exclusive access across processes using SET NX with a TTL and a unique token. It is not perfectly safe under network partitions, so it suits best-effort coordination, not correctness-critical mutual exclusion.', 'Always set a TTL to avoid a dead holder blocking forever, release only if you own the token (via a Lua check-and-delete), keep critical sections short, and use a database constraint when correctness truly matters.', 'A read-heavy API uses a Redis lock so only one worker refreshes an expensive cache entry, releasing it only if its token still matches.', 'Lock with NX plus TTL plus owner token; do not trust it for correctness.'),
    lesson('Redis Pub/Sub delivers messages to currently connected subscribers with no persistence, while Streams store an append-only log with consumer groups and acknowledgements. Pub/Sub loses messages if no one is listening; Streams do not.', 'Use Pub/Sub for fire-and-forget fan-out, use Streams with consumer groups for durable at-least-once processing, acknowledge processed entries, and trim streams to bound memory.', 'A read-heavy API broadcasts cache-invalidation events over Pub/Sub but uses a Stream with a consumer group for order events that must not be lost.', 'Pub/Sub forgets; Streams remember and acknowledge.'),
    lesson('Rate limiting protects a service by capping requests per client over time. Redis implements it efficiently with counters plus TTL (fixed window) or sorted sets (sliding window); the token-bucket pattern allows controlled bursts.', 'Choose the algorithm from burst tolerance, key by client identity, make the check atomic with a Lua script, and return clear limit headers so clients can back off.', 'A read-heavy API limits each API key to 100 requests per minute with an atomic Redis counter and returns a Retry-After header when the limit is hit.', 'Count per client atomically; choose the window for your burst policy.'),
    lesson('Redis persists with RDB snapshots, AOF logging, or both. Snapshots are compact but can lose recent writes; AOF loses less but is larger and slower to rewrite. A pure cache may need no persistence at all.', 'Match persistence to whether the data is a cache or a source of truth, use AOF with fsync tuning where durability matters, size memory for the rewrite, and test recovery time from the chosen format.', 'A read-heavy API runs its cache without persistence for speed, but a Redis instance used as a durable rate-limit store enables AOF and tests its restart recovery.', 'Persist only what you cannot rebuild; match the mode to durability need.'),
    lesson('Redis scales reads and availability with replication and Sentinel, and scales data with Cluster mode that shards keys across nodes by hash slot. Cluster changes multi-key operation semantics because keys must share a slot to be used together.', 'Use replicas and Sentinel or managed failover for availability, use hash tags to co-locate related keys in Cluster, avoid cross-slot multi-key commands, and monitor replication lag and slot balance.', 'A read-heavy API co-locates a user’s related cache keys with a hash tag so multi-key operations stay on one shard in Redis Cluster.', 'Replicate for availability, shard for size; keep related keys in one slot.'),
  ],
  'linux-networking': [
    lesson('A Linux process owns an address space and resources; threads share that process state. The scheduler, signals, file descriptors, cgroups, and namespaces shape what the process can do.', 'Inspect process state, CPU, memory, open files, syscalls, and cgroup limits before restarting. A high load average can mean runnable or uninterruptible tasks, not only CPU use.', 'A service latency spike is traced to processes waiting on blocked disk I/O rather than a shortage of CPU cores.', 'PID identifies; state explains; limits constrain.'),
    lesson('Unix permissions combine owner, group, mode bits, ACLs, and directory traversal rights. A user needs execute permission on every parent directory to reach a file.', 'Use least privilege, dedicated service users, safe umask, and explicit ownership. Diagnose with namei, stat, id, getfacl, and audit logs instead of chmod 777.', 'A deployment fixes a read failure by granting the service group directory traversal and file read access, not world write access.', 'Directory execute means traverse; file execute means run.'),
    lesson('TCP provides an ordered byte stream with connection state, retransmission, flow control, and congestion control; DNS maps names to records with caching and TTL. Application messages still need their own framing and timeout rules.', 'Trace name resolution, address family, handshake, TLS, retransmits, socket state, and return path. Use dig, ss, tcpdump, and connection metrics.', 'Intermittent API failures are caused by stale DNS in a long-lived client pool; TTL-aware refresh fixes the actual path rather than increasing retries.', 'Resolve name, connect transport, negotiate TLS, exchange framed application data.'),
    lesson('Resource analysis uses saturation and contention, not just average utilization. CPU run queue, memory pressure, swapping, disk latency, file descriptors, sockets, and cgroup throttling can each cap a service.', 'Correlate top-level symptoms with per-process and kernel evidence using ps, top, vmstat, iostat, pidstat, ss, and pressure stall information.', 'A container shows low average CPU but high throttled time because its quota is exhausted during bursts; adjusting work and limits removes tail latency.', 'Utilization says busy; saturation says work is waiting.'),
    lesson('Production troubleshooting should preserve evidence and reduce the search space: establish impact and timeline, compare healthy instances, follow dependencies, form one hypothesis, then run the safest discriminating test.', 'Capture logs, metrics, traces, process state, network evidence, and recent change history. Mitigate safely, document the cause, and add detection plus prevention.', 'A connection incident is mitigated by draining one bad node, then proven to be ephemeral-port exhaustion from missing client reuse.', 'Scope, compare, hypothesize, test, mitigate, prevent.'),
    lesson('The shell composes small programs with pipes, redirection, and exit codes. A robust script checks exit statuses, quotes variables, and fails fast, because a silent failure in automation is worse than a loud one.', 'Use set -euo pipefail, quote variables to survive spaces, check exit codes, prefer explicit tools over fragile parsing, and keep scripts idempotent so re-running is safe.', 'A deployment script sets -euo pipefail and quotes paths so a missing variable aborts loudly instead of running rm on the wrong directory.', 'Quote variables, check exit codes, fail fast.'),
    lesson('systemd manages services as units with dependencies, restart policies, resource limits, and journald logging. Running a daemon in a bare loop without systemd loses restart-on-failure, ordering, and log integration.', 'Define unit dependencies and restart policy, apply resource limits, read logs with journalctl, and use systemctl status to see why a service failed rather than guessing.', 'A production service runs as a systemd unit that restarts on failure, waits for the network, and logs to journald, so a crash recovers automatically and leaves evidence.', 'Let systemd own lifecycle, restart, limits, and logs.'),
    lesson('A load balancer or reverse proxy distributes traffic across backends and terminates TLS; it also affects timeouts, health checks, connection reuse, and how client IPs are seen. Misconfigured timeouts and health checks cause more outages than the backends themselves.', 'Align proxy and backend timeouts, use real dependency-aware health checks, preserve the client IP with forwarded headers, and enable keep-alive to avoid connection churn.', 'A service fixes intermittent 502s by raising the proxy read timeout above the backend’s slow endpoint and enabling upstream keep-alive.', 'Align timeouts, health-check dependencies, reuse connections.'),
    lesson('TLS provides encryption and identity through certificates signed by a trusted CA. Expired certificates, wrong hostnames, and incomplete chains are frequent outage causes, and certificates must be rotated before they expire.', 'Automate issuance and renewal (e.g. ACME), serve the full chain, match the certificate to the hostname, and monitor expiry dates with alerts well ahead of time.', 'A service avoids an outage because a certificate-expiry alert fires two weeks early and automated renewal replaces the cert before it lapses.', 'Automate renewal, serve the full chain, alert before expiry.'),
    lesson('A firewall controls which traffic is allowed by rules over source, destination, port, and protocol. Default-deny with explicit allows is safer than default-allow, and overly broad rules quietly expand the attack surface.', 'Default-deny inbound, allow only required ports from required sources, restrict management access, log denied traffic, and review rules so temporary openings do not become permanent.', 'A production host exposes only the application port to the load balancer and SSH to a bastion, denying everything else by default.', 'Default-deny; allow only what is required, from where it is required.'),
  ],
  docker: [
    lesson('A Docker image is an immutable content-addressed set of layers plus metadata; a container adds a writable layer and runtime isolation. Image layers are shared, so build order affects cache reuse and size.', 'Pin base images, copy dependency manifests before source, use multi-stage builds, inspect image history, and rebuild for security fixes rather than patching running containers.', 'A service compiles in a builder stage and copies only the binary and certificates into a small non-root runtime image.', 'Image is the recipe result; container is one running instance.'),
    lesson('Docker networking gives containers isolated interfaces, DNS-based service discovery on user-defined networks, port publishing, and host or overlay connectivity. localhost inside a container refers to that container.', 'Inspect names, routes, published ports, listeners, DNS, and host firewalls. Bind services deliberately and avoid exposing internal databases to every host interface.', 'An API connects to postgres:5432 by service name on a private network while only the API port is published to the host.', 'Container localhost is not host localhost.'),
    lesson('Container writable files disappear with replacement. Use volumes for managed local persistence, bind mounts for explicit host paths, and object or database services for durable shared data.', 'Define ownership, backup, restore, portability, and concurrent-access semantics. Never assume an image rebuild preserves runtime data.', 'Local PostgreSQL uses a named volume with tested backups; uploaded documents go to object storage so replicas can be replaced freely.', 'Container filesystem is disposable; durable state needs an owner.'),
    lesson('Container security reduces privileges and attack surface but is not a magical sandbox. Run as non-root, drop capabilities, use read-only filesystems where possible, scan images, and keep secrets outside layers.', 'Pin trusted bases, patch through rebuilds, use seccomp/AppArmor, limit resources, and never mount the Docker socket into an untrusted workload.', 'A web service runs with a numeric non-root UID, no extra capabilities, a read-only root filesystem, and a writable temporary mount.', 'Small image, small privilege, small writable surface.'),
    lesson('Fast Docker builds depend on cache-friendly layers, a small build context, deterministic dependencies, and multi-stage outputs. Smaller images improve transfer and attack surface but should not sacrifice debuggability blindly.', 'Use .dockerignore, BuildKit cache mounts and secret mounts, pinned dependencies, and CI checks for size and vulnerabilities. Verify the final stage contains no build secrets.', 'A Node build caches npm artifacts, copies lockfiles first, and excludes tests and local modules from the build context.', 'Stable inputs first; changing source later; runtime gets only outputs.'),
    lesson('Docker Compose defines multi-container applications declaratively with services, networks, volumes, and dependencies. It is ideal for local development and simple deployments, but depends_on waits for start, not for a service being ready.', 'Define service dependencies and health checks, use a private network for inter-service traffic, keep environment config in the compose file or env files, and wait on readiness rather than assuming start order.', 'A service runs its API, PostgreSQL, and Redis with one compose file, using health checks so the API waits until the database is actually ready.', 'Compose wires services locally; wait for ready, not just started.'),
    lesson('A registry stores and distributes images by name and tag or immutable digest. Mutable tags like latest cause non-reproducible deploys, while pinning by digest guarantees the exact image that was tested runs in production.', 'Tag with versions and deploy by digest, scan images in the registry, use private registries with authentication for internal images, and clean up old images to control storage.', 'A team promotes the exact image digest that passed CI through staging to production, so latest never causes an unexpected version to deploy.', 'Push by tag, deploy by digest, scan before release.'),
    lesson('Containers share the host kernel, so resource limits (CPU, memory) prevent one container from starving others. Without limits, a memory leak in one container can take down every workload on the host.', 'Set memory and CPU limits from measured usage, know that exceeding the memory limit triggers an OOM kill, leave headroom, and monitor actual usage against limits.', 'A service sets a memory limit so a leaking container is OOM-killed and restarted instead of crashing the whole host.', 'Limit CPU and memory; an unbounded container endangers its neighbors.'),
    lesson('Containers should log to stdout and stderr so the runtime collects them, rather than writing to files inside the ephemeral filesystem. Centralized log collection and basic metrics make containerized services observable.', 'Log structured JSON to stdout, let the platform ship logs to a central store, expose health and metrics endpoints, and avoid logging inside the container where logs vanish on restart.', 'A service logs structured JSON to stdout so the platform ships it to central logging, and exposes a health endpoint the orchestrator probes.', 'Log to stdout; let the platform collect and centralize it.'),
    lesson('Debugging containers means inspecting a running or crashed container: logs, exec into a shell, inspect config, and check exit codes. A container that exits immediately usually has a failing entrypoint or missing dependency, not a Docker bug.', 'Read docker logs and the exit code first, exec in to inspect state, use docker inspect for config and mounts, and add a shell or debugging tools to a temporary image when the runtime image is minimal.', 'A crash-looping container is diagnosed by reading its logs and exit code, revealing a missing environment variable rather than an image problem.', 'Logs and exit code first, then exec and inspect.'),
  ],
  kubernetes: [
    lesson('A Pod is the smallest scheduled unit and shares network and volumes among its containers. Deployments manage replaceable stateless replicas; StatefulSets add stable identity and ordered storage semantics; Jobs represent completion.', 'Set requests and limits from evidence, use probes for correct purposes, handle termination, and choose a controller matching lifecycle. A pod is disposable even when its workload matters.', 'A stateless API uses a Deployment, while a one-time migration uses a Job with explicit retry and timeout behavior.', 'Choose controller by lifecycle: serve, retain identity, or finish.'),
    lesson('A Service gives stable virtual discovery over changing pods; ingress or Gateway API routes external HTTP traffic. Readiness decides which pods receive traffic, while network policies constrain allowed paths.', 'Trace DNS, service selectors, endpoints, readiness, ingress rules, TLS, and policies. Avoid debugging the application before proving the network path.', 'A rollout sends traffic only to ready API pods through a ClusterIP Service and an HTTPS gateway with managed certificates.', 'Name resolves to Service; selector finds ready endpoints; policy permits the path.'),
    lesson('ConfigMaps hold non-secret configuration and Secrets hold sensitive values in the Kubernetes API, but base64 is not encryption. Workloads need a deliberate update and rotation strategy.', 'Use external secret integration where appropriate, encrypt etcd, restrict RBAC, avoid environment leaks, checksum mounted config for rollouts, and test rotation.', 'A platform mounts short-lived database credentials from an external secret provider and rolls pods when non-secret feature configuration changes.', 'Separate config from secret; plan propagation and rotation.'),
    lesson('Kubernetes security spans identity, RBAC, admission, pod security, images, network, nodes, and secrets. Namespaces organize scope but do not create full isolation by themselves.', 'Use least-privilege service accounts, restricted pod security, signed/scanned images, network policies, resource quotas, and audited admin access.', 'A tenant worker cannot call the Kubernetes API, runs without root or capabilities, and can connect only to its queue and database.', 'Secure identity, workload, network, supply chain, and control plane.'),
    lesson('Reliable Kubernetes workloads need correct probes, disruption budgets, topology spread, autoscaling signals, graceful shutdown, capacity headroom, and tested failure recovery.', 'Measure availability, latency, errors, saturation, restart causes, pending pods, and rollout status. Test node loss and dependency failure rather than assuming replication is resilience.', 'A service keeps replicas across zones, drains in-flight work on SIGTERM, and blocks rollout progress when error rate rises.', 'Replicas help only when failure domains and shutdown behavior are correct.'),
    lesson('The scheduler places pods on nodes based on resource requests, node selectors, affinity, and taints/tolerations. Requests drive placement, so wrong requests cause either wasted capacity or pods that cannot schedule.', 'Set accurate requests, use affinity and anti-affinity to spread or co-locate deliberately, use taints to reserve nodes, and use topology spread constraints to survive zone loss.', 'A platform uses pod anti-affinity to keep API replicas on different nodes so a single node failure never takes down all of them.', 'Requests drive placement; affinity and spread control where pods land.'),
    lesson('Kubernetes autoscaling has three axes: the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler adds pods, the Vertical Pod Autoscaler adjusts requests, and the Cluster Autoscaler adds nodes. Scaling on the wrong metric or without headroom causes lag during spikes.', 'Scale horizontally on a leading signal (CPU, or custom/queue metrics), ensure the cluster autoscaler can add nodes, set sane min/max, and leave headroom so scale-up is not too late.', 'A platform scales its workers on queue depth with the HPA, and the cluster autoscaler adds nodes when pending pods cannot fit.', 'Scale pods on a leading metric; make sure nodes can follow.'),
    lesson('Kubernetes storage uses PersistentVolumes and PersistentVolumeClaims with StorageClasses for dynamic provisioning. Access modes and the fact that most block storage is single-node ReadWriteOnce shape what stateful workloads can do.', 'Use StatefulSets with volume claim templates for stable per-pod storage, pick access modes that match the workload, understand reclaim policy, and back up persistent data outside the cluster.', 'A platform runs its database as a StatefulSet with per-pod ReadWriteOnce volumes and backs them up to object storage rather than relying on the cluster.', 'Match access mode to workload; back up persistent data off-cluster.'),
    lesson('Helm packages Kubernetes manifests into versioned, parameterized charts with templated values and release history. It reduces copy-paste across environments, but over-templating produces charts no one can read or debug.', 'Parameterize only what varies per environment, keep templates readable, pin chart and image versions, review rendered output, and use rollback to recover a bad release.', 'A platform deploys the same chart to staging and production with different values files and rolls back a bad release with one Helm command.', 'Template what varies; keep charts readable and versioned.'),
    lesson('Kubernetes observability combines metrics (often Prometheus), logs, and traces with cluster-specific signals like pod restarts, pending pods, and rollout status. A green deployment is not proof that users are healthy.', 'Scrape workload and cluster metrics, alert on user symptoms and saturation, watch restart and OOM counts, and correlate rollout events with error-rate changes.', 'A platform links a spike in 5xx errors to a specific rollout and pod restart storm, then rolls back before the error budget is exhausted.', 'Watch user symptoms plus cluster signals; tie errors to rollouts.'),
  ],
  'cicd-infrastructure': [
    lesson('A delivery pipeline turns one reviewed commit into a tested, traceable artifact promoted through environments. Build once and promote the same artifact to avoid environment-specific drift.', 'Order fast deterministic checks before expensive ones, isolate untrusted contributions, retain provenance, and make failures actionable. Do not place production secrets in build jobs that do not need them.', 'Several services build signed images once, run unit and integration checks, then promote digests through staging and production approvals.', 'Commit to artifact to evidence to promotion.'),
    lesson('Terraform declares desired infrastructure and compares it with state and provider reality. State is sensitive coordination data; modules should represent stable reusable boundaries, not hide every resource.', 'Use remote locked state, reviewed plans, pinned providers, import existing resources, and small blast-radius states. Never edit state casually or apply an unreviewed surprise plan.', 'A team separates shared networking state from each service, so one application change cannot accidentally replace the production VPC.', 'Configuration declares; state remembers; plan explains; apply changes.'),
    lesson('CI/CD secrets should be short-lived, narrowly scoped, auditable, and injected only into the job that needs them. Federated workload identity is safer than long-lived cloud keys.', 'Masking is not protection if a tool can exfiltrate a secret. Restrict fork workflows, rotate credentials, pin actions, and prevent secrets from entering images, caches, logs, or artifacts.', 'A deployment job exchanges its verified repository identity for a short-lived cloud token instead of storing an administrator key in CI.', 'Prefer identity exchange over stored credentials.'),
    lesson('Rolling, blue-green, and canary deployments trade capacity, speed, rollback, and exposure. Database and event changes must remain compatible across old and new versions during transition.', 'Define automated health gates from user metrics, bound canary exposure, preserve artifact rollback, and use expand-migrate-contract for schemas.', 'A canary receives 5% of traffic and advances only when latency and error budgets remain healthy; an incompatible schema cleanup waits until all old code is gone.', 'Deploy code gradually; evolve data compatibly.'),
    lesson('Delivery observability connects a commit and artifact to rollout events and service outcomes. A green pipeline proves checks passed, not that users are healthy.', 'Track deployment frequency, lead time, failure rate, recovery time, rollout duration, and post-deploy SLO signals. Record who promoted what and provide one-command rollback or roll-forward guidance.', 'When errors rise, the team links traces to the new image digest, pauses promotion, and rolls back while retaining evidence for diagnosis.', 'Trace commit to artifact to deployment to user impact.'),
    lesson('Testing in the pipeline layers fast unit tests, then integration and contract tests, then a few end-to-end checks, ordered so cheap checks fail first. A pipeline with only slow flaky tests gives slow, untrusted feedback.', 'Run fast deterministic tests early, parallelize slow suites, quarantine and fix flaky tests rather than retrying blindly, and require the checks that actually catch regressions before merge.', 'A team runs unit tests in under two minutes on every push and gates merges on integration tests, keeping end-to-end checks for the release candidate.', 'Fast checks first; kill flakiness; gate on tests that catch real regressions.'),
    lesson('Artifact and registry management means building once and promoting the same immutable, versioned artifact through environments, with provenance. Rebuilding per environment reintroduces the drift the pipeline was meant to remove.', 'Build once, tag by version and digest, sign artifacts and record provenance, store them in a registry with retention, and promote by digest rather than rebuilding.', 'A team builds a signed container image once, and promotes that exact digest from staging to production so what shipped is what was tested.', 'Build once, sign, promote the same digest everywhere.'),
    lesson('Environment promotion moves a tested artifact through stages (dev, staging, production) with gates and approvals, keeping configuration external so only config differs between environments. Divergent environments make staging tests meaningless.', 'Keep environments as similar as practical, externalize per-environment config and secrets, gate production with approvals, and promote the same artifact rather than a fresh build.', 'A team promotes one build through staging, runs smoke tests, then requires a manual approval before the same artifact reaches production.', 'Promote one artifact; only config should differ per environment.'),
    lesson('Infrastructure drift is when real infrastructure diverges from the declared configuration, usually from manual changes. Undetected drift makes the next apply surprising or destructive, so drift should be detected and reconciled.', 'Run plan or drift detection on a schedule, alert on unexpected differences, restrict manual console changes, and reconcile by codifying the change rather than editing state by hand.', 'A team catches a manually opened security-group rule through scheduled Terraform drift detection and reconciles it back into code.', 'Detect drift on a schedule; codify changes, do not hand-edit.'),
    lesson('Rollback and recovery restore a good state fast when a deploy goes wrong, through redeploying a previous artifact, reversing traffic, or restoring data. A deploy without a tested rollback is a one-way risk.', 'Keep previous artifacts deployable, use deployment strategies that allow fast reversal, make schema changes backward compatible so code can roll back, and rehearse the rollback path.', 'A team reverses a canary and redeploys the prior image within minutes because the artifact is retained and the schema change was backward compatible.', 'Keep the previous version deployable; make rollback rehearsed and safe.'),
  ],
  'system-design': [
    lesson('System design begins by separating functional behavior from measurable quality constraints: users, operations, data, scale, latency, availability, durability, security, compliance, and cost.', 'Quantify normal and peak load, data size, growth, and recovery objectives. State assumptions and identify the few constraints that actually shape architecture.', 'A notification platform first defines delivery channels, peak fan-out, latency SLO, duplicate tolerance, and regional failure objectives before drawing services.', 'Requirements first; numbers reveal the architecture.'),
    lesson('APIs and service boundaries should follow cohesive capabilities and ownership, not individual database tables. Contracts need idempotency, pagination, versioning, authentication, authorization, and failure semantics.', 'Prefer a modular monolith until independent scaling, failure isolation, ownership, or deployment cadence justifies distribution. Keep internal data private to its owner.', 'A notification capability owns preferences and delivery orchestration behind one API rather than creating a service for every endpoint.', 'Boundary by capability; distribute only for a measurable reason.'),
    lesson('Data architecture chooses sources of truth, models, indexes, replication, partitioning, retention, and event flows from access and consistency requirements. Every duplicate copy needs an update and repair story.', 'Document transactional boundaries, idempotency, ordering, backfills, privacy deletion, backups, and restore tests. Prefer constraints over application hope.', 'A notification request is durable in one relational source, while delivery workers consume an outbox event and build a repairable read model.', 'For each datum: owner, readers, consistency, retention, recovery.'),
    lesson('Scaling means finding and relieving the first constrained resource while preserving correctness. Stateless replicas help compute, partitioning helps distribution, caching helps repeated reads, and queues absorb temporary rate mismatch.', 'Use load estimates, capacity tests, saturation metrics, backpressure, admission control, and a degradation plan. Avoid adding distributed machinery before proving the bottleneck.', 'A global activity feed starts with read replicas and cache, then partitions only after measured write concentration exceeds the primary’s safe headroom.', 'Measure the bottleneck; scale that resource; preserve backpressure.'),
    lesson('Every architecture trades consistency, latency, availability, complexity, cost, and operability. A strong design names the rejected alternatives and the threshold that would change the decision.', 'Run failure-mode reviews and threat models, define SLOs and budgets, estimate cost drivers, test recovery, and keep migration paths reversible where uncertainty is high.', 'A platform chooses at-least-once delivery with idempotent consumers over expensive global exactly-once claims and documents the duplicate-rate objective.', 'Decision, trade-off, failure, proof, change threshold.'),
    lesson('A caching strategy trades freshness for latency and load reduction, and must be designed with the source of truth, the tolerated staleness, and the invalidation path. A cache without an invalidation story serves wrong data eventually.', 'Cache at the layer that gives the most benefit (client, CDN, application, database), define TTLs and invalidation on writes, protect against stampedes, and never cache personalized data in a shared tier.', 'A notification platform caches public template metadata at the CDN and edge, while per-user delivery state stays uncached and authoritative in the database.', 'Cache by staleness tolerance; every cache needs an invalidation path.'),
    lesson('Load balancing spreads traffic across instances for scale and availability, at layer 4 (transport) or layer 7 (application). The algorithm, health checks, and session handling determine whether load is even and failures are hidden.', 'Use health checks that reflect real readiness, choose an algorithm from the traffic shape, keep services stateless so any instance can serve any request, and account for connection draining on deploys.', 'A notification platform runs stateless API instances behind a layer-7 load balancer with dependency-aware health checks so a failing instance is removed automatically.', 'Balance across stateless instances; health-check real readiness.'),
    lesson('Rate limiting and quotas protect a system from overload and abuse and enforce fair usage across tenants. Global limits protect the system; per-client limits ensure one tenant cannot starve others.', 'Limit per client and globally, choose an algorithm (token bucket for bursts, sliding window for smoothness), return clear limit headers, and degrade gracefully rather than collapsing under overload.', 'A notification platform caps each tenant’s send rate and applies a global limit, returning Retry-After so a burst from one tenant does not delay every other tenant.', 'Per-client limits for fairness; global limits for survival.'),
    lesson('Consistency and replication trade data freshness against availability and latency. Synchronous replication keeps replicas current but adds latency and can block on failure; asynchronous replication is faster but can lose recent writes and serve stale reads.', 'Choose per-workload: strong consistency for invariants, eventual for scale, read-after-write where users expect their own changes, and make staleness visible where it exists.', 'A notification platform stores delivery records with strong consistency but serves the activity feed from asynchronous replicas with a visible freshness indicator.', 'Replicate for scale; spend synchronous consistency only on invariants.'),
    lesson('Observability and SLOs define what healthy means and prove it with the golden signals: latency, traffic, errors, and saturation. An SLO with an error budget turns reliability into a measurable, negotiable target instead of a vague goal.', 'Define user-centric SLOs, measure the golden signals, alert on error-budget burn rather than raw thresholds, and use tracing to attribute latency across services.', 'A notification platform sets a 99.9% delivery SLO, alerts when the error budget burns too fast, and uses traces to find which stage of the pipeline caused a regression.', 'Define SLOs on user experience; alert on budget burn.'),
  ],
  'distributed-systems': [
    lesson('Distributed consistency describes what values operations may observe across replicas and time. Stronger guarantees simplify reasoning but add coordination latency and reduce availability during partitions.', 'State invariants and read-after-write needs per operation, not for the whole product. Use versioning, quorum, or transactions where needed and tolerate staleness deliberately elsewhere.', 'A financial balance requires serializable updates, while an analytics dashboard accepts delayed replicas with a visible freshness timestamp.', 'Spend coordination only on invariants that require it.'),
    lesson('Messaging decouples producers and consumers in time, but common brokers provide at-least-once delivery. Consumers must handle duplicates, reordering, poison messages, backpressure, and schema evolution.', 'Use transactional outbox for database-to-message reliability, idempotency keys, bounded retries, dead-letter handling, lag metrics, and replay procedures.', 'A payment service commits an outbox event with its transaction; the ledger consumer deduplicates by event ID before applying it.', 'At least once plus idempotency beats magical exactly once.'),
    lesson('Resilience contains partial failure through timeouts, retries with jitter, circuit breakers, bulkheads, queues, and graceful degradation. Each mechanism can amplify failure if unbounded or layered carelessly.', 'Set end-to-end deadlines, retry only safe transient operations, cap attempts, preserve capacity for recovery, and test dependency slowdown—not just hard failure.', 'When a fraud provider slows, payment requests use a short bounded retry and route eligible low-risk cases to manual review instead of exhausting every request thread.', 'Timeout first; retry safely; isolate capacity; degrade deliberately.'),
    lesson('Distributed observability reconstructs one user operation across services using correlated structured logs, metrics, traces, and events. Clock skew and sampling mean no single signal is complete.', 'Propagate trace and request IDs, measure golden signals and queue lag, record deployment versions, redact sensitive data, and retain enough evidence for replay and audit.', 'A trace shows a financial workflow waiting in a queue after one consumer deployment, confirmed by lag and version metrics.', 'Follow causality across request, message, dependency, and deployment.'),
    lesson('Threat modeling identifies assets, trust boundaries, actors, entry points, abuse cases, and mitigations before incidents. Security includes confidentiality, integrity, availability, and accountable recovery.', 'Authenticate workloads and users, authorize each resource action, encrypt transport and storage, minimize secrets and privileges, validate messages, rate-limit abuse, and test incident response.', 'A financial workflow signs provider webhooks, rejects replay, scopes service identity to one topic, and preserves an auditable immutable event record.', 'Asset, actor, boundary, abuse, control, detection, recovery.'),
    lesson('Consensus algorithms like Raft let a group of nodes agree on a value or ordered log despite failures, providing the foundation for leader election and replicated state. Consensus is expensive, so use a proven system rather than building your own.', 'Use an established coordinator (etcd, ZooKeeper, or a managed equivalent) for leader election and configuration, understand quorum needs an odd number of nodes, and avoid putting consensus on a hot data path.', 'A financial workflow elects a single active scheduler through etcd so exactly one instance runs periodic settlement, failing over automatically if the leader dies.', 'Agree via quorum with a proven system; keep it off the hot path.'),
    lesson('In distributed systems there is no single global clock, so wall-clock time is unreliable for ordering events. Logical clocks and vector clocks establish causal order, and unsynchronized clocks cause subtle bugs in expiry and ordering.', 'Order events with logical clocks or sequence numbers, avoid relying on cross-node timestamps for correctness, use bounded clock skew assumptions carefully, and prefer server-assigned monotonic IDs.', 'A financial workflow orders ledger entries by a monotonic sequence rather than event timestamps, so clock skew between services cannot reorder transactions.', 'No global clock; order by logical sequence, not wall time.'),
    lesson('Partitioning (sharding) distributes data across nodes to scale beyond one machine, using a partition key. A poor key creates hot partitions, and operations spanning partitions lose the simplicity of single-node transactions.', 'Choose a key that spreads load and keeps related data together, plan for rebalancing, avoid cross-partition transactions where possible, and design queries around the partition key.', 'A financial workflow partitions accounts by account ID so each account’s transactions stay on one shard, keeping per-account operations single-partition.', 'Partition to spread load while keeping related data together.'),
    lesson('Idempotency lets an operation be safely retried without changing the result beyond the first application, which is essential because distributed systems retry. Deduplication tracks which requests were already processed.', 'Require idempotency keys for mutations, store processed keys to detect duplicates, make handlers naturally idempotent where possible, and set retention on the dedup store.', 'A financial workflow processes each payment event once by recording its idempotency key in a unique constraint, so a duplicate delivery is safely ignored.', 'Retries are inevitable; make operations idempotent and dedupe by key.'),
    lesson('Disaster recovery restores service after a major failure such as a region outage or data corruption, measured by recovery point objective (data loss) and recovery time objective (downtime). A plan that has never been tested is not a plan.', 'Replicate data across failure domains, back up with tested restores, define and rehearse failover, keep runbooks current, and match the DR investment to the RPO/RTO the business actually requires.', 'A financial workflow replicates its ledger to a second region and runs an annual failover drill, proving it can meet its recovery-time objective before a real outage.', 'Define RPO/RTO, replicate across domains, and rehearse recovery.'),
  ],
  'behavioral-hr': [
    lesson('Behavioral answers use past behavior as evidence, so a strong story separates situation, responsibility, personal action, result, and reflection. The story should be specific enough for follow-up questions without becoming a long chronology.', 'Choose one relevant event, keep the setup brief, say “I” for your contribution and “we” for team outcomes, explain the difficult choice, quantify or describe the result, and finish with what you learned.', 'During a checkout incident, I owned customer communication while another engineer led recovery. I shortened update intervals, translated technical risk for support, and helped reduce duplicate escalations; afterward I added an incident-communication checklist.', 'Context briefly, responsibility clearly, action personally, result visibly, reflection honestly.'),
    lesson('Motivation and career-fit answers connect verified facts about the role and organization to the candidate’s skills, values, and next learning goal. Genuine alignment is stronger than flattery or saying only that the company is successful.', 'Name two specific reasons for the role, connect each to credible past evidence, explain the contribution you can make now, and state what you hope to learn without implying that the employer exists only for your career.', 'I am interested in the role because the team owns customer-facing reliability and works across product and engineering. I have improved incident communication in my current team, and I want to deepen that skill while contributing from the first quarter.', 'Specific role, credible connection, immediate contribution, honest growth.'),
    lesson('A credible strength is a repeatable behavior supported by evidence; a credible weakness is a real limitation that is relevant but manageable. The weakness answer should show impact, ownership, a concrete improvement system, and current progress.', 'For a strength, name the behavior, prove it with one outcome, and explain where it helps. For a weakness, avoid disguised strengths, describe the consequence, show the habit or feedback loop you changed, and do not claim the issue has vanished.', 'My strength is making ambiguous work concrete: on a delayed integration I wrote the decision log and acceptance examples that aligned three teams. My weakness was waiting too long to escalate; I now set risk checkpoints and raise blocked dependencies with options before deadlines move.', 'Strength needs evidence; weakness needs ownership, action, and progress.'),
    lesson('Strong teamwork and conflict answers show respect, direct communication, shared goals, and the ability to disagree without turning the other person into the problem. The goal is not to prove that the candidate always wins.', 'Describe the disagreement neutrally, listen and restate the other concern, identify the shared objective, compare evidence and constraints, involve the decision owner when needed, commit to the decision, and explain the relationship and outcome afterward.', 'A designer and I disagreed about delaying a release for accessibility fixes. I clarified the affected keyboard journey, listened to the campaign deadline, and proposed shipping the compliant core flow while deferring a nonessential animation; we met the date without blocking keyboard users.', 'Disagree on the work, align on the goal, decide with evidence, commit together.'),
    lesson('Time management is prioritization under limited capacity, not fitting unlimited work into a calendar. Strong answers explain how impact, urgency, dependencies, risk, and effort shape the order, and how trade-offs are communicated before deadlines fail.', 'List commitments, clarify real deadlines and decision owners, rank by impact and dependency, reserve focus time and risk buffer, limit work in progress, review the plan as facts change, and renegotiate scope or dates early with options.', 'When two launches and an urgent customer defect collided, I confirmed the defect’s revenue impact, paused a low-risk reporting task, split one launch into must-have and follow-up scope, and sent owners a revised plan the same day; the defect and core launch shipped on time.', 'Prioritize impact, expose trade-offs, limit work in progress, communicate early.'),
    lesson('Leadership and influence answers show how a candidate moves work forward and aligns people without necessarily having formal authority. Credible influence comes from clarity, evidence, and building trust, not from title or forcing a decision.', 'Describe the goal and the people involved, explain how you built shared understanding, used data and listening to align, and gave others credit; show the outcome and that the relationship stayed healthy.', 'Without being the lead, I unblocked a stalled integration by writing a one-page proposal comparing two options with cost and risk, walking each team through it, and adopting a colleague’s improvement; the teams aligned in a week and shipped on schedule.', 'Influence through clarity, evidence, and trust, not authority.'),
    lesson('Failure and learning answers use a real mistake to show ownership and growth, not a disguised humblebrag. A strong answer names the failure honestly, explains the impact, what the candidate specifically did to recover, and the durable change they made.', 'Choose a genuine failure, take personal ownership without blaming others, describe the impact and recovery, and end with the concrete practice you changed so it does not recur; avoid claiming you now never fail.', 'I once shipped a change without a rollback plan and caused an hour of downtime. I owned the incident, led the fix, and wrote the postmortem; since then I require a tested rollback before any risky deploy, which has prevented two later incidents.', 'Own the failure, show the recovery, prove the lasting change.'),
    lesson('Handling feedback and criticism answers show that a candidate can hear hard feedback without defensiveness and act on it. Interviewers look for genuine reflection and a concrete change, not a rehearsed claim that all feedback is welcome.', 'Describe the feedback plainly, resist justifying, explain how you evaluated whether it was fair, the specific action you took, and the result; show that you separated the feedback from your ego.', 'A manager told me my design reviews were too terse and left juniors confused. Instead of defending my style, I asked for examples, started explaining the why behind comments, and later a junior said reviews had become their main learning source.', 'Listen without defending, act specifically, show the result.'),
    lesson('Stakeholder communication answers show a candidate can translate between technical and non-technical audiences, set expectations, and deliver hard news early. The skill is tailoring the message to what each audience needs to decide.', 'Explain who the stakeholders were, what each needed to know, how you adjusted the level of detail, and how you communicated risk or bad news early with options rather than surprises.', 'When a launch was slipping, I gave leadership a short risk summary with two options and a recommendation, and gave engineers the detailed blockers; the early, tailored update let leadership choose a scoped launch instead of discovering the slip at the deadline.', 'Tailor the message to the audience; deliver risk early with options.'),
    lesson('Salary and offer negotiation answers show that a candidate can advocate for themselves professionally, backed by research and framed collaboratively. The goal is a fair outcome and a good relationship, not winning at the employer’s expense.', 'Research the market range, anchor on your value with evidence, state a clear number or range calmly, stay collaborative rather than adversarial, and consider the whole package, not just base salary.', 'When I received an offer below my research range, I thanked them, shared the market data and my relevant impact, and asked whether they could move toward the midpoint; we agreed on a number close to it and I started on good terms.', 'Advocate with research and value, stay collaborative, weigh the whole package.'),
  ],
};
